1
00:00:14,014 --> 00:00:18,051
We're here today
with Cho Yeo-jeong and Ha Seok-jin.

2
00:00:18,118 --> 00:00:21,187
Not only is Seok-jin a great actor,

3
00:00:21,254 --> 00:00:23,556
but he's known
as one of the smartest actors out there.

4
00:00:23,623 --> 00:00:26,393
Hanyang University's engineering major,
an intellectual heartthrob, so to say.

5
00:00:26,459 --> 00:00:27,527
-Intellectual heartthrob.
-Yes.

6
00:00:27,594 --> 00:00:29,729
Also, he was the winner
of <i>The Devil's Plan,</i>

7
00:00:29,796 --> 00:00:31,898
<i>a strategy-driven survival game show.</i>

8
00:00:31,965 --> 00:00:33,633
-He has receipts.
-I remember.

9
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:36,469
-One of our chefs studied engineering too.
-Is that so?

10
00:00:36,536 --> 00:00:38,972
A guy who studied
at Rose-Hulman Institute of Technology.

11
00:00:39,239 --> 00:00:40,206
WE KNOW THAT GUY

12
00:00:40,273 --> 00:00:41,107
Who could it be?

13
00:00:41,174 --> 00:00:43,676
"Rose-Hulman."

14
00:00:43,743 --> 00:00:45,712
-It's probably Chef Jong-won.
-Jong-won?

15
00:00:45,779 --> 00:00:49,049
-He studied engineering in the States.
-Jong-won.

16
00:00:49,115 --> 00:00:51,384
I didn't graduate, though. I dropped out.

17
00:00:51,451 --> 00:00:52,786
You switched to culinary arts.

18
00:00:52,852 --> 00:00:54,821
I'm sure you had a particular major.

19
00:00:54,888 --> 00:00:57,223
I majored in civil engineering
and optical engineering.

20
00:00:57,290 --> 00:00:58,892
You must've studied mechanics.

21
00:00:58,958 --> 00:01:00,260
I DID

22
00:01:00,326 --> 00:01:02,962
-It deals with streets and bridges.
-What about you, Seok-jin?

23
00:01:03,029 --> 00:01:04,764
-I majored in mechanical engineering.
-Mechanical engineering.

24
00:01:04,831 --> 00:01:06,332
We probably studied
a few similar subjects.

25
00:01:06,399 --> 00:01:08,034
HOW COOL

26
00:01:08,101 --> 00:01:10,236
-I also attended a vo-tech school.
-Good for you.

27
00:01:11,071 --> 00:01:11,938
Vo-tech school?

28
00:01:12,005 --> 00:01:13,406
JUNG-HWAN WANTED TO JOIN THEM

29
00:01:13,807 --> 00:01:16,042
I didn't think
you'd suddenly chime in like that.

30
00:01:16,109 --> 00:01:18,144
We probably studied
a few of the same subjects.

31
00:01:18,211 --> 00:01:20,914
He went to a vo-tech high
but majored in business at college.

32
00:01:20,980 --> 00:01:23,149
INTERDISCIPLINARY TALENT

33
00:01:23,216 --> 00:01:24,918
I studied architecture in high school.

34
00:01:24,984 --> 00:01:26,086
BLUSHING A BIT

35
00:01:26,152 --> 00:01:27,554
Did you study mechanics too?

36
00:01:27,620 --> 00:01:29,556
You didn't pick up skills there, did you?

37
00:01:29,622 --> 00:01:31,591
I often used triangle rulers too.

38
00:01:31,658 --> 00:01:32,492
LIKE THIS

39
00:01:32,559 --> 00:01:34,160
-The angle's important.
-I always had to.

40
00:01:34,227 --> 00:01:36,963
That's all I ever did
while learning about architecture.

41
00:01:37,030 --> 00:01:38,898
Your family could've hoped

42
00:01:38,965 --> 00:01:40,233
you'd make use of your major

43
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:43,803
-and gotten a job instead.
-Yes, instead of getting into acting.

44
00:01:43,870 --> 00:01:46,372
I had a chance
to dip a toe into the industry,

45
00:01:46,439 --> 00:01:48,575
so my family told me to try it for a year,

46
00:01:48,641 --> 00:01:50,944
see if I like doing that job, and if not,

47
00:01:51,010 --> 00:01:52,879
I could always go back to studying.

48
00:01:52,946 --> 00:01:54,814
My agency built on the momentum
after my debut

49
00:01:54,881 --> 00:01:57,283
and suggested that I continue
instead of returning to school.

50
00:01:58,718 --> 00:02:01,154
You two are similar in that
while you were in college,

51
00:02:01,221 --> 00:02:04,157
-you decided to change courses.
-They did.

52
00:02:04,224 --> 00:02:05,792
And one of them is a real chef,

53
00:02:05,859 --> 00:02:07,760
-and the other one will play one.
-Exactly.

54
00:02:07,827 --> 00:02:09,829
Mechanical engineering
and civil engineering.

55
00:02:09,896 --> 00:02:11,397
And me, architecture.

56
00:02:11,831 --> 00:02:14,000
THE FORMER STRIKER
FINDS HIS CHANCE TO SLIDE IN

57
00:02:14,067 --> 00:02:15,101
I don't get me either.

58
00:02:15,168 --> 00:02:16,870
EXCELLENT, THAT WAS SMOOTH

59
00:02:16,936 --> 00:02:19,873
But I guess there's this thing.
People who study this field

60
00:02:19,939 --> 00:02:22,442
-like to fiddle with tools.
-So true.

61
00:02:22,509 --> 00:02:23,877
We learn to use tools easily

62
00:02:23,943 --> 00:02:26,379
and tend to fix broken things quickly too.

63
00:02:26,446 --> 00:02:29,182
When kitchen tools break at work,
I tell my staff

64
00:02:29,249 --> 00:02:31,651
not to send it anywhere for repairs
because I do it myself.

65
00:02:31,718 --> 00:02:34,154
So you're really like that?

66
00:02:34,220 --> 00:02:36,222
They have that kind of pride.

67
00:02:36,289 --> 00:02:39,492
-So I've thrown out a number of tools.
-Right.

68
00:02:39,559 --> 00:02:42,128
-I always send it out to have it fixed.
-Always?

69
00:02:42,195 --> 00:02:44,797
You'll be playing a chef
on your upcoming project.

70
00:02:44,864 --> 00:02:47,033
And chefs come
in many different characters.

71
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:49,969
Some are attentive, some can be snippy.

72
00:02:50,036 --> 00:02:53,072
He's overall quite attentive,

73
00:02:53,139 --> 00:02:55,675
but he can be quite snippy in the kitchen.

74
00:02:55,742 --> 00:02:58,077
A level-headed character.

75
00:02:58,144 --> 00:03:02,015
I want to observe the chefs today
and pick up what I can.

76
00:03:02,081 --> 00:03:04,150
-Then I had a question.
-What is it?

77
00:03:04,217 --> 00:03:06,052
Chef Sam

78
00:03:06,119 --> 00:03:09,422
always has a smile on his face
and is so warm-hearted.

79
00:03:09,489 --> 00:03:13,326
So does a guy like him
also change in the kitchen,

80
00:03:13,393 --> 00:03:16,796
become difficult,
and even swear when he works?

81
00:03:16,863 --> 00:03:19,532
Yes, I'm very difficult.

82
00:03:19,599 --> 00:03:21,668
He changes completely. Look at his canine.

83
00:03:21,734 --> 00:03:23,203
I hear he bites people.

84
00:03:23,269 --> 00:03:25,305
-Give us an example.
-What are you like when you're mad?

85
00:03:25,371 --> 00:03:28,308
-He may have to act angry in the kitchen.
-I had a scene, actually.

86
00:03:28,374 --> 00:03:32,145
The fridge broke down
and the ingredients all got spoiled,

87
00:03:32,212 --> 00:03:34,714
but none of my staff members caught on.

88
00:03:34,781 --> 00:03:37,884
-So I had to go and berate them.
-They must be reprimanded.

89
00:03:37,951 --> 00:03:41,921
But the level of anger I should express
was solely based on my interpretation.

90
00:03:41,988 --> 00:03:44,958
-I had lots of room, so I began to think.
-That's something…

91
00:03:45,024 --> 00:03:47,460
-…that'll make you completely lose it.
-Completely?

92
00:03:47,760 --> 00:03:48,861
FOCUSED

93
00:03:48,928 --> 00:03:50,430
It's about management.

94
00:03:50,496 --> 00:03:53,266
I'd go, "How come no one knew about this?"

95
00:03:53,333 --> 00:03:55,935
-It can't be like that. That's not anger.
-That's not getting angry.

96
00:03:56,002 --> 00:03:57,003
HESITANT

97
00:03:57,070 --> 00:03:59,372
I get really furious
when something like that happens.

98
00:03:59,439 --> 00:04:01,107
Yes, so how?

99
00:04:01,174 --> 00:04:03,176
-Don't just explain it.
-It's like this.

100
00:04:03,243 --> 00:04:06,112
I gather all of them and go,
"How come no one knew?"

101
00:04:06,179 --> 00:04:07,413
-So you berate them.
-No.

102
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:09,515
Don't smile and do it
like you're actually there.

103
00:04:09,582 --> 00:04:10,583
Just be yourself and go…

104
00:04:10,650 --> 00:04:12,185
OH, YOU ENDEARING GUY

105
00:04:12,919 --> 00:04:14,687
YOU CUTE LITTLE THING, HUH?

106
00:04:15,021 --> 00:04:18,091
COMING LIVE FROM THE SCENE

107
00:04:18,391 --> 00:04:20,660
NOW, THAT'S WHAT HE NEEDS TO DO

108
00:04:20,727 --> 00:04:22,629
If you ever get mad in the kitchen,

109
00:04:22,695 --> 00:04:24,697
please take a video and show us.

110
00:04:24,764 --> 00:04:26,666
I'll study him through the CCTV footage.

111
00:04:26,733 --> 00:04:29,002
Chefs, do you have any tips for him

112
00:04:29,068 --> 00:04:31,237
to portray a chef more realistically?

113
00:04:31,304 --> 00:04:33,039
In my case, I often use tools like scales,

114
00:04:33,106 --> 00:04:35,908
<i>like precision scales and rulers.</i>

115
00:04:35,975 --> 00:04:36,909
<i>As an engineering major.</i>

116
00:04:36,976 --> 00:04:38,645
But in the kitchen,

117
00:04:38,711 --> 00:04:42,649
-I think Nam-no is on the active side.
-Yes.

118
00:04:42,715 --> 00:04:44,350
<i>His actions are big, making him look cool.</i>

119
00:04:44,417 --> 00:04:47,320
Any tips, Chef Nam-no?

120
00:04:47,387 --> 00:04:48,488
Well, actually…

121
00:04:48,554 --> 00:04:50,123
CHEWING…

122
00:04:50,189 --> 00:04:51,591
-Stop eating the gummies.
-How many gummies have you had?

123
00:04:51,658 --> 00:04:55,495
-You're still munching on those?
-It's been hours.

124
00:04:55,561 --> 00:04:57,196
He finished almost everything.

125
00:04:57,263 --> 00:04:58,898
NO ATE THE PILE OF GUMMIES

126
00:04:58,965 --> 00:05:01,000
When you look at ingredients
like onions and carrots,

127
00:05:01,067 --> 00:05:02,802
don't look at them like this.

128
00:05:02,869 --> 00:05:04,537
If you're to act it out,

129
00:05:04,604 --> 00:05:07,874
you must glare upward like this
and give them a serious look.

130
00:05:07,940 --> 00:05:09,042
That's the secret?

131
00:05:09,108 --> 00:05:10,843
Don't we call that wild-eyed?

132
00:05:10,910 --> 00:05:11,811
Like this.

133
00:05:11,878 --> 00:05:13,079
NOW, LOOK!

134
00:05:13,780 --> 00:05:15,515
SERIOUS LOOK

135
00:05:16,115 --> 00:05:16,949
OBSERVING SERIOUSLY

136
00:05:17,016 --> 00:05:18,418
It looks like he's picking a fight.

137
00:05:18,484 --> 00:05:20,720
And throw a raw piece
in your mouth for no reason.

138
00:05:20,787 --> 00:05:23,156
THAT WAS AN EXCUSE TO EAT ANOTHER GUMMY…

139
00:05:23,723 --> 00:05:25,158
EUREKA

140
00:05:25,224 --> 00:05:26,726
It's to check.

141
00:05:26,793 --> 00:05:28,628
TRIES IT RIGHT AWAY

142
00:05:28,695 --> 00:05:30,163
-Glare up at it.
-Glare up.

143
00:05:30,229 --> 00:05:32,432
He's looking weird because of you.

144
00:05:32,498 --> 00:05:34,734
COPYING YOON NAM-NO

145
00:05:35,535 --> 00:05:37,737
LET ME TRY!

146
00:05:37,804 --> 00:05:39,739
-It turned into a comedy piece.
-Wait.

147
00:05:39,806 --> 00:05:43,209
-Did you say you wanted to meet Eun-yeong?
-You said that?

148
00:05:43,276 --> 00:05:46,979
I watched the show and noticed
that unlike when I was last on the show,

149
00:05:47,046 --> 00:05:50,016
-people began to dance a lot.
-You're right.

150
00:05:50,083 --> 00:05:52,452
But the moment she began to dance,

151
00:05:52,518 --> 00:05:54,454
she owned the studio.

152
00:05:54,821 --> 00:05:56,989
THE BEGINNING OF HER MAD EYES ERA

153
00:05:57,056 --> 00:06:00,026
-Her eyes suddenly shifted drastically.
-And it's just her.

154
00:06:00,093 --> 00:06:03,329
So I wanted to use that
for my performance.

155
00:06:03,396 --> 00:06:07,166
What kind of signal must I give
to my brains to show those eyes?

156
00:06:07,233 --> 00:06:09,469
-I began to study her…
-He took it as art.

157
00:06:09,535 --> 00:06:11,571
…but I couldn't copy it.

158
00:06:11,637 --> 00:06:14,474
It's something you'd call
split personality,

159
00:06:14,540 --> 00:06:15,742
playing two characters at once.

160
00:06:15,808 --> 00:06:17,009
NOTICES HER

161
00:06:17,076 --> 00:06:18,611
Why are you laughing so hard?

162
00:06:18,678 --> 00:06:21,581
I'm also an actor,
so I had a similar thought.

163
00:06:21,647 --> 00:06:23,182
"I should copy her."

164
00:06:23,249 --> 00:06:25,017
-Copy her?
-What's going on, Eun-yeong?

165
00:06:25,084 --> 00:06:27,053
So she has a knack for acting?

166
00:06:27,120 --> 00:06:29,689
What's the process
that's going on in your head?

167
00:06:29,756 --> 00:06:31,391
You become one with it

168
00:06:31,457 --> 00:06:32,892
and just accept it.

169
00:06:32,959 --> 00:06:34,861
-Accept who?
-From what I've seen,

170
00:06:34,927 --> 00:06:37,630
it's not that
she puts in a certain neural signal.

171
00:06:37,697 --> 00:06:41,501
She has to sever something in her brain.

172
00:06:41,567 --> 00:06:42,769
A neurectomy.

173
00:06:42,835 --> 00:06:45,071
UNDERSTOOD

174
00:06:45,138 --> 00:06:47,640
Eun-yeong, did you
also want to meet Seok-jin?

175
00:06:47,707 --> 00:06:51,277
Yes, my husband is known
to be his lookalike.

176
00:06:51,344 --> 00:06:52,779
DISAPPROVES

177
00:06:52,845 --> 00:06:54,947
I've seen lots of articles pop up,

178
00:06:55,014 --> 00:06:57,083
and of course, I became curious.

179
00:06:57,150 --> 00:06:59,552
I looked him up to see
how similar we looked

180
00:06:59,619 --> 00:07:00,953
<i>but I couldn't find a picture of his face.</i>

181
00:07:01,020 --> 00:07:03,623
<i>Right. He's not a public figure.</i>

182
00:07:03,689 --> 00:07:06,793
I hear he goes around wearing a mask
after hearing that he looks like him.

183
00:07:06,993 --> 00:07:08,961
REALLY?

184
00:07:09,028 --> 00:07:12,932
He said he felt embarrassed
to go around without a mask,

185
00:07:12,999 --> 00:07:15,768
<i>because people may go,</i>
<i>"Let's see if he looks like Ha Seok-jin."</i>

186
00:07:15,835 --> 00:07:17,303
<i>That's why he wears a mask.</i>

187
00:07:18,237 --> 00:07:22,108
He looks like a gentler version of him.

188
00:07:22,175 --> 00:07:25,044
But the vibes they give off seem similar.

189
00:07:25,111 --> 00:07:26,212
THEY LOOK SIMILAR IN HER EYES

190
00:07:26,279 --> 00:07:29,115
I was at her wedding,
so why didn't I pick up on that vibe?

191
00:07:29,182 --> 00:07:31,150
-I see.
-Right?

192
00:07:31,217 --> 00:07:33,252
Nam-no, did you pick it up?

193
00:07:34,253 --> 00:07:35,087
No.

194
00:07:36,422 --> 00:07:38,458
-He must've been busy eating.
-He never stopped.

195
00:07:38,524 --> 00:07:39,892
Maybe it was Jee Seok-jin.

196
00:07:39,959 --> 00:07:42,261
-Jee Seok-jin?
-He's handsome.

197
00:07:42,328 --> 00:07:43,830
He said he looked like Jee Seok-jin.

198
00:07:43,896 --> 00:07:45,698
WHY ARE YOU BRINGING HIM UP?

199
00:07:47,767 --> 00:07:48,868
He's good-looking.

200
00:07:48,935 --> 00:07:51,137
I can't do the show with him.
He's so annoying.

201
00:07:51,204 --> 00:07:53,039
Her husband is good-looking, though.

202
00:07:53,105 --> 00:07:54,707
And so is Jee Seok-jin.

203
00:07:54,774 --> 00:07:56,375
-All right.
-That went pretty far.

204
00:07:56,442 --> 00:07:57,977
-Doesn't he, though?
-Seriously. Not at all.

205
00:07:58,044 --> 00:07:59,178
I can't tell by this picture.

206
00:07:59,245 --> 00:08:00,947
-I kind of get it.
-You get it, right?

207
00:08:01,013 --> 00:08:02,982
-I get what you mean.
-It's his hairstyle.

208
00:08:03,049 --> 00:08:04,884
-No.
-But honestly, no, not at all.

209
00:08:04,951 --> 00:08:06,786
-I'm serious.
-It's this part.

210
00:08:06,853 --> 00:08:08,821
-Yes, this part.
-And the dimples.

211
00:08:09,322 --> 00:08:13,426
This is the second time having
Seok-jin on <i>Chef & My Fridge.</i>

212
00:08:13,493 --> 00:08:17,630
-It's been 11 years since his last visit.
<i>-Hello, I'm Ha Seok-jin.</i>

213
00:08:17,697 --> 00:08:20,132
Since the last time I was here,

214
00:08:20,199 --> 00:08:22,034
I've aged quite a bit

215
00:08:22,101 --> 00:08:24,170
so my taste in food changed too.

216
00:08:25,171 --> 00:08:26,806
When I'd go on dates,

217
00:08:26,873 --> 00:08:31,310
I used to stick with Western cuisine,
such as pasta and whatnot.

218
00:08:31,377 --> 00:08:34,514
But these days,
I prefer <i>eobok-jaengban, </i>hot pot platter.

219
00:08:34,580 --> 00:08:36,916
Or <i>baeksuk</i> with shingled
hedgehog mushrooms.

220
00:08:36,983 --> 00:08:39,719
-I'm craving shingled hedgehogs.
-Your appetite changed a lot.

221
00:08:39,785 --> 00:08:43,656
I think I'm in the process
of becoming an <i>ajae,</i> an old dude.

222
00:08:43,723 --> 00:08:46,592
I hear you talk to yourself often,

223
00:08:46,659 --> 00:08:49,161
-Seok-jin.
-Really?

224
00:08:49,228 --> 00:08:52,365
-That's what old dudes do.
-Only old guys do that.

225
00:08:52,431 --> 00:08:54,300
I find myself mumbling.

226
00:08:54,367 --> 00:08:55,701
"What's in the fridge?"

227
00:08:55,768 --> 00:08:58,704
-I find myself adding melodies too.
-It happens.

228
00:08:58,771 --> 00:09:00,039
Even as I drive, I go,

229
00:09:00,106 --> 00:09:02,675
"The traffic is heavy."

230
00:09:02,742 --> 00:09:05,444
-Yes, you're right.
-I keep finding myself doing that.

231
00:09:05,511 --> 00:09:07,680
I often chat with my GPS navigator.

232
00:09:07,747 --> 00:09:11,584
It goes, "Turn left."
I go, "Gotta turn left. Gotcha."

233
00:09:11,651 --> 00:09:15,555
-Yes, we do talk to ourselves a lot.
-My gosh.

234
00:09:15,621 --> 00:09:18,958
Jong-won, are there moments when you go,
"Have I become an old dude?"

235
00:09:19,025 --> 00:09:21,360
I work with lots of Gen MZ people, right?

236
00:09:21,427 --> 00:09:22,695
When I make a joke,

237
00:09:23,262 --> 00:09:26,132
at times, you hear people laughing,
and you can tell that it was genuine.

238
00:09:26,999 --> 00:09:28,267
But at other times, it's faked.

239
00:09:28,334 --> 00:09:29,335
FORCED LAUGHTER

240
00:09:29,402 --> 00:09:31,604
I keep hearing laughs like these.

241
00:09:31,671 --> 00:09:33,339
-"You're so funny."
-"How funny."

242
00:09:33,406 --> 00:09:34,307
"Really?"

243
00:09:35,308 --> 00:09:37,109
I don't think
it's that kind of a dad joke.

244
00:09:37,176 --> 00:09:39,545
They call my jokes dad jokes

245
00:09:39,612 --> 00:09:42,148
because they go,
"Oh, there's the dead joke again."

246
00:09:42,715 --> 00:09:43,983
COLD SILENCE

247
00:09:44,150 --> 00:09:46,252
HAHAHAHA
(GUYS, JUST LAUGH FOR NOW)

248
00:09:46,319 --> 00:09:49,322
-He's funny.
-I'm laughing because he's the head chef.

249
00:09:49,789 --> 00:09:52,224
WE CAN'T HELP BUT APPLAUD, CHEF

250
00:09:52,291 --> 00:09:53,726
I do it to lighten up the mood.

251
00:09:53,793 --> 00:09:54,961
He's so funny.

252
00:09:55,027 --> 00:09:56,596
He doesn't give a darn.

253
00:09:56,662 --> 00:09:59,765
-Once you get older, you really don't.
-Now that you mention it,

254
00:09:59,832 --> 00:10:01,701
once I've become older,

255
00:10:01,767 --> 00:10:06,038
I find myself standing on the balcony,
staring at the scenery.

256
00:10:06,472 --> 00:10:08,207
POV: A WEEKEND MORNING FOR DADS

257
00:10:08,274 --> 00:10:10,343
I stare out for ages
with hands clasped to the back.

258
00:10:10,409 --> 00:10:12,478
-It happens.
-I didn't do that when I was younger.

259
00:10:12,545 --> 00:10:14,714
THE GRANDPA CAN RELATE

260
00:10:14,780 --> 00:10:17,883
Sung-jun, you're yet to feel old, right?

261
00:10:17,950 --> 00:10:21,520
The thing is, legally speaking,
people born in 1995 are still YCC.

262
00:10:21,587 --> 00:10:22,922
So I'm not really sure.

263
00:10:22,989 --> 00:10:25,024
So ask Nam-no, who's now an OCC.

264
00:10:25,091 --> 00:10:26,359
What now? Young…

265
00:10:26,425 --> 00:10:28,394
-What's that?
-What now?

266
00:10:28,461 --> 00:10:30,162
-YCC?
-What's YCC?

267
00:10:30,229 --> 00:10:32,732
I also recently found out
from my kitchen staff.

268
00:10:32,798 --> 00:10:36,902
I saw them
chatting happily with one another,

269
00:10:36,969 --> 00:10:38,704
and they go,
"You're too OCC to know, right?"

270
00:10:38,771 --> 00:10:40,740
-You wouldn't know.
-OCC? Too old?

271
00:10:40,806 --> 00:10:42,642
-Exactly.
-So there are YCC and OCC,

272
00:10:42,708 --> 00:10:44,944
but I was too embarrassed to ask.

273
00:10:45,011 --> 00:10:47,613
-It's a recent meme.
-YCC, OCC.

274
00:10:47,680 --> 00:10:49,081
OCC, YCC.

275
00:10:49,148 --> 00:10:51,784
Yeo-jeong was greatly shocked to find out

276
00:10:51,851 --> 00:10:53,486
that Sung-jun was born in 1995.

277
00:10:53,552 --> 00:10:55,254
I wasn't surprised.

278
00:10:56,522 --> 00:10:57,690
Not at all.

279
00:10:57,757 --> 00:11:00,126
-She's acting.
-I'm YCC. YoungCreatorCrew.

280
00:11:00,192 --> 00:11:01,694
Sure, YCC.

281
00:11:01,761 --> 00:11:04,563
Born in 1995. It's fascinating.

282
00:11:04,630 --> 00:11:08,834
Seok-jin is really into Japanese ramen,

283
00:11:09,535 --> 00:11:12,605
so he goes around trying different places.

284
00:11:12,672 --> 00:11:15,174
If I were into pasta or Chinese food,

285
00:11:15,241 --> 00:11:17,510
I would've needed a group to go with.

286
00:11:17,576 --> 00:11:20,346
But all I need to order
is a single bowl for ramen,

287
00:11:20,413 --> 00:11:22,481
-so it's easier to go alone.
-Right.

288
00:11:23,382 --> 00:11:25,951
I'm sure you have a list of good places.

289
00:11:26,018 --> 00:11:29,155
I always take a picture
of the bowl whenever I eat,

290
00:11:29,221 --> 00:11:33,125
and at times,
even a video to show the soup's texture.

291
00:11:33,192 --> 00:11:37,897
If you see a photo, do you recall
where you had it and what it was like?

292
00:11:37,963 --> 00:11:42,702
I probably remember most places
I've visited in the last two, three years.

293
00:11:42,768 --> 00:11:45,137
That's how
an intellectual heartthrob works.

294
00:11:45,204 --> 00:11:49,608
We asked him beforehand
and received some photos

295
00:11:49,675 --> 00:11:51,143
from Seok-jin.

296
00:11:51,210 --> 00:11:54,013
Let's see how many places he has been to.

297
00:11:55,815 --> 00:11:56,649
That's a lot.

298
00:11:56,716 --> 00:11:58,818
-This is all ramen.
-Yes.

299
00:11:58,884 --> 00:11:59,985
That's a lot.

300
00:12:00,052 --> 00:12:01,020
An awful lot.

301
00:12:01,087 --> 00:12:02,655
Did you eat some of them overseas?

302
00:12:02,722 --> 00:12:04,890
They're mostly domestic.
Maybe a few places in Japan.

303
00:12:04,957 --> 00:12:06,726
There are that many ramen places in Seoul?

304
00:12:06,792 --> 00:12:09,361
There are lots.
I go to lots of places too.

305
00:12:09,428 --> 00:12:10,262
Really?

306
00:12:10,329 --> 00:12:12,364
Let's pick out a place and see…

307
00:12:12,431 --> 00:12:14,400
-If he remembers.
-…if he remembers.

308
00:12:14,467 --> 00:12:17,670
We'll pick out something randomly.

309
00:12:17,737 --> 00:12:19,538
Why don't we go up to the screen
and point at one?

310
00:12:20,940 --> 00:12:22,875
That's so analog.

311
00:12:22,942 --> 00:12:24,643
-Then they'll enlarge it.
-Do it, vo-tech high kid.

312
00:12:24,710 --> 00:12:25,678
I'll pick one at random.

313
00:12:25,745 --> 00:12:27,780
-Let the architecture major go.
-The vo-tech high kid.

314
00:12:27,847 --> 00:12:30,750
-"Let the architecture major go"?
-Yes.

315
00:12:30,816 --> 00:12:33,185
-This one looks unique.
-I can't see.

316
00:12:33,252 --> 00:12:34,453
Yes, that's new.

317
00:12:34,520 --> 00:12:37,156
The meat is hanged on the edge.

318
00:12:37,223 --> 00:12:39,091
-Yes, over the edge.
-Right?

319
00:12:39,825 --> 00:12:41,260
I think that's from

320
00:12:41,327 --> 00:12:45,731
K restaurant in Nonhyeon-dong.
They did that with their ramen.

321
00:12:45,798 --> 00:12:48,167
-He really remembers.
-Probably.

322
00:12:48,234 --> 00:12:50,803
The one to the right of that
is from Eunpyeong-gu,

323
00:12:50,870 --> 00:12:52,571
M restaurant.

324
00:12:52,638 --> 00:12:54,140
-This one is Eunpyeong-gu?
-Yes.

325
00:12:54,206 --> 00:12:55,775
The one on the right of that

326
00:12:55,841 --> 00:12:57,877
is H place in Yeonnam-dong.

327
00:12:57,943 --> 00:13:00,112
They give you all this roasted seaweed?

328
00:13:00,179 --> 00:13:01,413
Yes, I like to add extra.

329
00:13:01,480 --> 00:13:02,748
-I see.
-It was extra.

330
00:13:02,815 --> 00:13:04,316
Like a nobleman's hat.

331
00:13:05,551 --> 00:13:07,820
THE LAUGHTER OF PEOPLE ACTUALLY LAUGHING

332
00:13:07,887 --> 00:13:09,021
Can only nobles have it?

333
00:13:09,088 --> 00:13:11,624
That's W place near Gangnam Station.

334
00:13:11,690 --> 00:13:14,026
-So it's mostly like that.
-He remembers everything.

335
00:13:14,093 --> 00:13:15,628
How does he remember all that?

336
00:13:15,694 --> 00:13:18,497
He's an intellectual heartthrob
and a ramen heartthrob.

337
00:13:18,564 --> 00:13:21,133
And now, we're about to open

338
00:13:21,200 --> 00:13:23,803
Seok-jin's fridge again after 11 years.

339
00:13:23,869 --> 00:13:25,805
Back then, I remember

340
00:13:25,871 --> 00:13:28,474
<i>-that he was really into beer.</i>
<i>-Homebrewed.</i>

341
00:13:28,541 --> 00:13:32,178
-Yes, he brewed it at home or something.
-Yes.

342
00:13:32,244 --> 00:13:33,946
Do you still do that?

343
00:13:34,013 --> 00:13:36,916
No, I don't make anything anymore.

344
00:13:36,982 --> 00:13:39,185
-It's vain.
-It's all in vain.

345
00:13:39,251 --> 00:13:41,287
You have that kind of passion
when you're younger.

346
00:13:41,353 --> 00:13:43,222
I'm kind of scared

347
00:13:43,289 --> 00:13:45,591
because when they took that fridge,

348
00:13:45,658 --> 00:13:48,160
I was on set, not home.

349
00:13:48,227 --> 00:13:50,896
I should've been there
and told them to leave some stuff.

350
00:13:50,963 --> 00:13:52,698
But you couldn't.

351
00:13:52,765 --> 00:13:54,567
So literally everything is here.

352
00:13:54,633 --> 00:13:56,569
-This is exciting.
-It's pretty much empty.

353
00:13:56,635 --> 00:13:59,405
-It's the naked truth.
-It's like showing my underwear drawer.

354
00:13:59,471 --> 00:14:00,940
-That's nice.
-And we love that.

355
00:14:01,006 --> 00:14:02,341
We love a good search and seizure.

356
00:14:02,408 --> 00:14:06,145
-We love it when the homeowner isn't home.
-Exactly.

357
00:14:06,212 --> 00:14:08,747
We never misrepresent
what it actually looked like.

358
00:14:08,814 --> 00:14:10,649
Everything is exactly as it was.

359
00:14:10,716 --> 00:14:13,719
-It needs some misrepresentation.
-Oh, gosh.

360
00:14:14,353 --> 00:14:15,788
It's been 11 years.

361
00:14:15,855 --> 00:14:17,857
It's nice to see his fridge again.

362
00:14:17,923 --> 00:14:20,426
-We present Seok-jin's fridge!
-Fridge!

363
00:14:20,492 --> 00:14:22,494
-Whoa.
-He has lots of stuff.

364
00:14:22,561 --> 00:14:24,763
-We present Seok-jin's fridge!
-Fridge!

365
00:14:25,664 --> 00:14:28,234
LOOKING NEAT, UNLIKE HOW HE WAS WORRIED

366
00:14:28,801 --> 00:14:30,502
It looks neat.

367
00:14:30,569 --> 00:14:32,471
As expected, some delivery food.

368
00:14:32,538 --> 00:14:35,207
The first two shelves
are mostly delivery food.

369
00:14:35,274 --> 00:14:37,276
Did you have the entire fridge delivered?

370
00:14:38,244 --> 00:14:39,778
Let's see what he likes to order.

371
00:14:39,845 --> 00:14:41,580
-That's so relatable.
-Are these all ramen?

372
00:14:42,381 --> 00:14:43,849
<i>-Kimchi-jjigae.</i>
<i>-Kimchi-jjigae.</i>

373
00:14:43,916 --> 00:14:45,117
That's a lot left over.

374
00:14:45,184 --> 00:14:48,187
I divide it into portions
and warm it up in a smaller bowl.

375
00:14:48,254 --> 00:14:50,356
What's this one?

376
00:14:50,422 --> 00:14:52,324
-It's milky broth.
-Is it <i>dwaeji-gukbap?</i>

377
00:14:52,391 --> 00:14:54,393
Either <i>dwaeji-gukbap </i>or <i>seolleong-tang.</i>

378
00:14:54,460 --> 00:14:56,862
-It looks untouched.
-I haven't even opened it.

379
00:14:56,929 --> 00:14:59,632
I had to order some more
to reach the minimum amount.

380
00:14:59,698 --> 00:15:02,001
-For later.
-So I ordered another soup.

381
00:15:02,067 --> 00:15:05,104
-Tacos?
-Tacos.

382
00:15:05,170 --> 00:15:07,640
At times, I like to do
a collaboration of different cuisines.

383
00:15:07,706 --> 00:15:09,541
-A collaboration?
-A collaboration?

384
00:15:09,608 --> 00:15:11,810
-A mix-and-match.
-A fusion meal.

385
00:15:11,877 --> 00:15:13,846
It's fun to eliminate those leftovers

386
00:15:13,913 --> 00:15:15,581
like you're playing Tetris.

387
00:15:15,648 --> 00:15:17,983
I heard about a "Ha Seok-jin set"
in Chinese restaurants.

388
00:15:18,050 --> 00:15:19,451
-Ha Seok-jin set?
-What?

389
00:15:19,518 --> 00:15:21,020
-A while ago…
-How often did you have it?

390
00:15:21,086 --> 00:15:25,024
There are <i>jjajangmyeon </i>types
that serve the sauce on the side, right?

391
00:15:25,090 --> 00:15:27,226
And if you order <i>jjamppong</i> rice,

392
00:15:27,293 --> 00:15:28,861
you get lots of soup with it.

393
00:15:28,928 --> 00:15:31,730
So I'd split it up into three portions,

394
00:15:31,797 --> 00:15:35,467
-and the <i>jjajang </i>sauce is a lot too.
-Right.

395
00:15:35,534 --> 00:15:38,237
So first, I eat the soup and the rice,
then the soup and the noodles,

396
00:15:38,304 --> 00:15:39,972
and with the sauce next time.

397
00:15:40,039 --> 00:15:42,775
It's probably the way I split up
two bowls into different meals.

398
00:15:42,841 --> 00:15:44,143
SHARING HIS FORMULA

399
00:15:44,209 --> 00:15:46,178
His engineering brain is at work.

400
00:15:46,245 --> 00:15:48,647
I've always liked to mix up my meals.

401
00:15:48,714 --> 00:15:50,950
Now, the second shelf.

402
00:15:51,016 --> 00:15:53,152
-These must've been delivered too.
-Yes, the sides.

403
00:15:53,218 --> 00:15:55,521
I see some kimchi and pickles.

404
00:15:55,587 --> 00:15:57,056
You're thrifty.

405
00:15:57,122 --> 00:15:59,992
-Look at this stash.
-Probably Tabasco packets.

406
00:16:00,059 --> 00:16:02,394
-I like to keep them too.
-It's a shame to throw them out.

407
00:16:02,461 --> 00:16:04,930
-I don't want to throw them out.
-Yes, I have a stash too.

408
00:16:04,997 --> 00:16:07,433
What's this? What's this powder?

409
00:16:08,233 --> 00:16:09,635
That one?

410
00:16:09,702 --> 00:16:12,771
I had mountains of Parmesan
after ordering pizza,

411
00:16:13,605 --> 00:16:15,107
-so I gathered them.
-A great idea.

412
00:16:15,174 --> 00:16:16,842
Did you rip out those packets?

413
00:16:16,909 --> 00:16:18,711
Yes, and it's pretty full now.

414
00:16:18,777 --> 00:16:21,814
I like how thrifty you are
in saving all this food,

415
00:16:21,880 --> 00:16:22,748
but do you eat it all?

416
00:16:22,815 --> 00:16:24,616
I always make sure I pull out

417
00:16:24,683 --> 00:16:28,020
-ones I had delivered first.
-Right.

418
00:16:28,087 --> 00:16:29,621
First-in, first-out.

419
00:16:29,688 --> 00:16:31,256
You remember the order?

420
00:16:31,557 --> 00:16:32,925
BRAIN LOGICS

421
00:16:32,992 --> 00:16:36,095
The ones that arrive early go to the back
like drinks at a convenience store,

422
00:16:36,161 --> 00:16:38,197
so I pull out the ones that arrived first.

423
00:16:38,263 --> 00:16:40,432
You know which ones you ordered earlier,

424
00:16:40,499 --> 00:16:42,368
so you eat the older ones first.

425
00:16:42,434 --> 00:16:43,435
THE FRIDGE IS IN MY HEAD

426
00:16:44,203 --> 00:16:45,771
I find these things intriguing.

427
00:16:46,372 --> 00:16:47,973
It's like a typical home.

428
00:16:48,040 --> 00:16:49,541
You brought all those?

429
00:16:49,608 --> 00:16:50,876
-I should've stopped you.
-Oh, no.

430
00:16:50,943 --> 00:16:52,044
This isn't a lid.

431
00:16:52,111 --> 00:16:54,213
-That's a first.
-That's too realistic.

432
00:16:54,279 --> 00:16:56,949
-That's a dish.
-That's actually candid.

433
00:16:57,950 --> 00:16:59,184
SMH

434
00:16:59,251 --> 00:17:00,819
-What's in here?
-Let's see it.

435
00:17:00,886 --> 00:17:04,790
This is a bowl
Seok-jin left covered with a dish.

436
00:17:05,391 --> 00:17:06,725
Here it is.

437
00:17:06,792 --> 00:17:08,293
-What's that?
-Soup?

438
00:17:08,360 --> 00:17:09,995
It looks like broth.

439
00:17:10,062 --> 00:17:11,563
That's…

440
00:17:11,630 --> 00:17:13,031
-Jeez.
-What is it?

441
00:17:13,265 --> 00:17:14,433
WHAT IS IT?

442
00:17:14,500 --> 00:17:15,768
I made <i>suyuk,</i> boiled meat.

443
00:17:15,834 --> 00:17:19,438
I had some broth left
after making the dish,

444
00:17:19,505 --> 00:17:23,208
and the broth smelled so good,
although it tasted a bit strong.

445
00:17:23,275 --> 00:17:26,578
So I had it chilled
to mix it with something later.

446
00:17:27,312 --> 00:17:29,014
I should've thrown it out.

447
00:17:29,081 --> 00:17:30,849
There's one more.

448
00:17:30,916 --> 00:17:33,919
-Is this a saucer?
-A saucer?

449
00:17:33,986 --> 00:17:36,555
-It's a saucer for a teacup.
-You're right.

450
00:17:36,622 --> 00:17:38,957
-My mom used to do that.
-This was a regular dish.

451
00:17:39,024 --> 00:17:40,192
What's in here?

452
00:17:40,793 --> 00:17:41,627
Ta-da.

453
00:17:41,693 --> 00:17:42,694
-Lard?
-It's this.

454
00:17:42,761 --> 00:17:44,930
-It's beef fat.
-Beef fat.

455
00:17:44,997 --> 00:17:46,865
-You have beef fat?
-Beef fat?

456
00:17:46,932 --> 00:17:49,501
-Why is it red?
-Oh, well,

457
00:17:49,568 --> 00:17:51,737
when I left soup in the fridge
after it got delivered,

458
00:17:51,804 --> 00:17:53,972
the oil rose and hardened.

459
00:17:54,039 --> 00:17:56,241
-That layer.
-It's a mix of chili oil and beef fat.

460
00:17:56,308 --> 00:17:58,410
I usually ate it
after skimming the fat off,

461
00:17:59,078 --> 00:18:01,080
then I came to think,
"Maybe I can use this oil."

462
00:18:02,347 --> 00:18:03,715
He has that research spirit.

463
00:18:04,450 --> 00:18:06,485
You have that scientist side.

464
00:18:06,552 --> 00:18:08,520
Let's go to the bins on the side.

465
00:18:08,587 --> 00:18:09,455
NERVOUS

466
00:18:09,521 --> 00:18:11,890
-What's this?
-Wait.

467
00:18:11,957 --> 00:18:13,926
It looks as if he's getting interrogated.

468
00:18:13,992 --> 00:18:15,661
-Blueberry jam?
-No.

469
00:18:15,727 --> 00:18:18,330
-There's something else inside.
-It's something else?

470
00:18:19,598 --> 00:18:21,133
-How thrifty.
-It says blueberry jam.

471
00:18:21,200 --> 00:18:24,503
-What's that?
-It's probably some onion thing.

472
00:18:24,570 --> 00:18:25,971
-Onion?
-It smells like onion.

473
00:18:26,038 --> 00:18:28,607
-It's been a while. Look.
-At least three years, I say.

474
00:18:28,674 --> 00:18:32,010
Wait a second.
There can't be just one of these.

475
00:18:32,611 --> 00:18:35,047
What's this? Gosh. What is it?

476
00:18:35,114 --> 00:18:36,014
This expired

477
00:18:36,582 --> 00:18:37,816
in 2017?!

478
00:18:38,383 --> 00:18:39,218
In 2017?

479
00:18:39,284 --> 00:18:41,386
He should've had it before March 9, 2017.

480
00:18:41,453 --> 00:18:43,689
MIND-BOGGLING

481
00:18:43,755 --> 00:18:44,623
I get it's canned, but…

482
00:18:44,690 --> 00:18:46,258
It's probably pâté.

483
00:18:46,325 --> 00:18:48,127
-Pâté.
-Pâté.

484
00:18:48,193 --> 00:18:50,129
I'll hold onto it for nine more months

485
00:18:50,195 --> 00:18:51,463
and throw it out after a decade.

486
00:18:53,599 --> 00:18:54,833
What's this one?

487
00:18:54,900 --> 00:18:56,568
-What's that?
-It's new.

488
00:18:56,635 --> 00:18:59,138
Whoa, there's a life-form inside.

489
00:18:59,204 --> 00:19:00,539
-Life-form?
-There's a life-form?

490
00:19:00,606 --> 00:19:03,609
-See? There's a life-form moving inside.
-It's moving?

491
00:19:03,675 --> 00:19:05,410
I've seen something like this
in a science lab.

492
00:19:05,477 --> 00:19:07,179
It's <i>yeoneo-jang</i>, spicy marinated salmon.

493
00:19:07,246 --> 00:19:09,047
-<i>Yeoneo-jang?</i>
-<i>Yeoneo-jang?</i>

494
00:19:09,114 --> 00:19:11,750
Made on February 5th, 2021.

495
00:19:12,784 --> 00:19:16,088
Best by seven days
after the production date.

496
00:19:16,155 --> 00:19:18,056
OH MY GOODNESS

497
00:19:18,123 --> 00:19:20,125
You should've had it within seven days.

498
00:19:20,192 --> 00:19:21,160
Seven days?

499
00:19:21,226 --> 00:19:23,061
You'll have to throw this out.

500
00:19:23,128 --> 00:19:24,563
-I don't want you to open it.
-What?

501
00:19:24,863 --> 00:19:26,532
BUT I'M SO CURIOUS♥

502
00:19:26,598 --> 00:19:27,766
BUT WE

503
00:19:27,833 --> 00:19:28,700
REALLY AREN'T

504
00:19:28,767 --> 00:19:29,835
-Sung-ju.
-Yes?

505
00:19:29,902 --> 00:19:31,069
Can you open it in your seat?

506
00:19:33,071 --> 00:19:34,406
But Yeo-jeong is there.

507
00:19:34,773 --> 00:19:36,141
PROTECT YEO-JEONG AT ALL COST

508
00:19:36,208 --> 00:19:38,544
The rule of the show is that
whoever opens it has a bite.

509
00:19:38,610 --> 00:19:39,711
Yes.

510
00:19:39,778 --> 00:19:40,879
That's our ironclad rule.

511
00:19:40,946 --> 00:19:42,714
-Try the sauce.
-Let's check its condition.

512
00:19:42,781 --> 00:19:45,350
-We all told you not to open it.
-It's new.

513
00:19:45,417 --> 00:19:47,085
Why would you open it?

514
00:19:47,152 --> 00:19:48,654
It's going to blow up.

515
00:19:48,720 --> 00:19:50,289
-Be careful.
-Look at that color.

516
00:19:50,355 --> 00:19:52,291
-I'm scared.
-Why do I feel itchy?

517
00:19:52,357 --> 00:19:53,258
I'm scared.

518
00:19:53,325 --> 00:19:54,927
Whoa, the gas.

519
00:19:54,993 --> 00:19:56,094
Hide me.

520
00:19:56,161 --> 00:19:56,995
The gas.

521
00:19:57,062 --> 00:19:57,896
It's open.

522
00:19:59,631 --> 00:20:01,099
SAFE

523
00:20:01,166 --> 00:20:02,167
It'd be <i>jeotgal</i> by now.

524
00:20:02,234 --> 00:20:03,969
It turned gray.

525
00:20:04,036 --> 00:20:05,637
-The salmon.
-That's scary.

526
00:20:05,704 --> 00:20:08,740
These are salmon slices.

527
00:20:08,807 --> 00:20:10,776
So Seok-jin had this side?

528
00:20:10,842 --> 00:20:12,511
-You don't throw things out.
-Is it thriftiness?

529
00:20:12,578 --> 00:20:14,813
I never really go through my fridge.

530
00:20:14,880 --> 00:20:16,381
-And throw this out.
-Everything should go.

531
00:20:16,448 --> 00:20:18,317
-This is going.
-And this one.

532
00:20:18,383 --> 00:20:20,485
Hey, check those too.

533
00:20:20,552 --> 00:20:23,222
This was good by November 4th, 2022.

534
00:20:23,288 --> 00:20:24,890
June 18th, 2023.

535
00:20:24,957 --> 00:20:26,325
Let's get rid of this bin.

536
00:20:27,226 --> 00:20:28,327
They were exiled.

537
00:20:28,393 --> 00:20:29,328
We'll throw these out.

538
00:20:29,394 --> 00:20:30,529
I didn't know it expired

539
00:20:30,596 --> 00:20:31,930
and had it once a few months ago.

540
00:20:31,997 --> 00:20:32,831
-What?
-This?

541
00:20:32,898 --> 00:20:35,000
But I didn't get sick.
I did throw it out after.

542
00:20:35,067 --> 00:20:37,102
Are you asking us not to throw these out?

543
00:20:37,169 --> 00:20:38,303
No, please do.

544
00:20:38,370 --> 00:20:40,339
I'm not sure if you want us to or not.

545
00:20:40,405 --> 00:20:42,541
-We must do it for his sake.
-We can't let him get sick.

546
00:20:42,608 --> 00:20:44,610
My immunity level
slowly seems to be rising.

547
00:20:45,310 --> 00:20:47,913
Do you enjoy drinking alone?
This is <i>makgeolli,</i> right?

548
00:20:47,980 --> 00:20:49,314
-And sake.
-This must be sake.

549
00:20:49,381 --> 00:20:50,916
Do you enjoy sake and <i>makgeolli?</i>

550
00:20:50,983 --> 00:20:53,785
Whenever I have to shoot
for a long time on set and variety shows,

551
00:20:53,852 --> 00:20:56,688
I'm in a state of hyperarousal,
and they help me calm down.

552
00:20:56,755 --> 00:20:58,657
It's my after-work drink.

553
00:20:59,725 --> 00:21:01,627
He'll need a lot today.

554
00:21:01,693 --> 00:21:04,196
-To the freezer, then?
-Let's look at the freezer.

555
00:21:04,263 --> 00:21:05,464
He has lots of stuff.

556
00:21:05,530 --> 00:21:07,199
-Sea urchin roe.
-Sea urchin roe.

557
00:21:07,266 --> 00:21:09,501
-He has sea urchin roe?
-Sea urchin roe.

558
00:21:09,568 --> 00:21:11,837
Those are fresh as h…

559
00:21:11,903 --> 00:21:13,105
I almost said "heck."

560
00:21:13,171 --> 00:21:15,140
WATCH OUT FOR HYPERAROUSAL

561
00:21:15,207 --> 00:21:19,211
I recently appeared on
another variety show

562
00:21:19,278 --> 00:21:20,879
and had sea urchin roe pasta.

563
00:21:21,513 --> 00:21:22,347
That sounds great.

564
00:21:22,414 --> 00:21:24,483
-You have bread in the freezer.
-Bread.

565
00:21:24,549 --> 00:21:27,319
My younger sister lives in Germany,

566
00:21:27,386 --> 00:21:29,788
so she sent it to me,
saying it's popular there.

567
00:21:29,855 --> 00:21:33,058
I left it in the freezer,
and it's been about a month.

568
00:21:33,125 --> 00:21:36,061
I still don't get
why people love plain bread.

569
00:21:36,128 --> 00:21:38,463
-Sounds good.
-What type of bread do you like?

570
00:21:38,530 --> 00:21:40,866
I like the ones that kids like.

571
00:21:40,932 --> 00:21:42,968
Things like pizza bread
or sausage bread.

572
00:21:43,035 --> 00:21:45,170
-Or red bean bread.
-Red bean bread.

573
00:21:45,237 --> 00:21:46,405
I love red bean paste.

574
00:21:46,471 --> 00:21:49,174
Red bean bread isn't for kids.
It's for old dudes.

575
00:21:49,608 --> 00:21:51,376
A-HA!

576
00:21:51,443 --> 00:21:54,079
-So you did cook before.
-He has dried seafood.

577
00:21:54,146 --> 00:21:55,714
These are dried seafood for broth.

578
00:21:55,781 --> 00:21:58,550
Anchovies and Dokdo shrimp.

579
00:21:58,617 --> 00:22:01,520
When I order <i>tsukemen,</i>
a type of dipping ramen,

580
00:22:01,586 --> 00:22:03,822
I like to eat it
with some powdered seafood.

581
00:22:03,889 --> 00:22:07,326
-It adds lots of flavors.
-It must be savory.

582
00:22:07,392 --> 00:22:09,361
You have lots of meat.

583
00:22:09,428 --> 00:22:10,462
Why do you have so much?

584
00:22:10,529 --> 00:22:13,432
-Pork belly.
-For bulgogi. Whoa.

585
00:22:13,498 --> 00:22:14,933
-Beef brisket.
-Right.

586
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:16,601
You must love meat.

587
00:22:16,668 --> 00:22:19,671
I got those as presents
and they're great quality.

588
00:22:19,738 --> 00:22:23,508
But I usually go out to Majang-dong
with my friends and eat meat there.

589
00:22:23,575 --> 00:22:24,710
So those are some leftovers

590
00:22:24,776 --> 00:22:27,112
because I couldn't eat them alone,
but they're good meat.

591
00:22:27,179 --> 00:22:29,681
It felt awkward
to grill the meat alone at home.

592
00:22:29,748 --> 00:22:32,050
What's the problem
with grilling it at home?

593
00:22:32,117 --> 00:22:34,086
I used to do it from time to time.

594
00:22:34,152 --> 00:22:36,521
But about two or three years ago,

595
00:22:36,588 --> 00:22:37,689
I looked up how to cook good meat

596
00:22:37,756 --> 00:22:40,359
on the day of a major holiday.

597
00:22:40,425 --> 00:22:42,728
So I heated up a pan

598
00:22:42,794 --> 00:22:45,397
to sear the meat front and back.

599
00:22:45,964 --> 00:22:47,933
Then the alarm went off
in the entire building.

600
00:22:47,999 --> 00:22:49,301
Because of the smoke?

601
00:22:49,368 --> 00:22:51,336
-The alarm?
-So the security guard came

602
00:22:51,403 --> 00:22:53,872
and banged on my door,
asking if I was okay.

603
00:22:53,939 --> 00:22:57,175
I caused a nuisance to my entire building
on a holiday.

604
00:22:57,242 --> 00:22:59,778
It was embarrassing.

605
00:22:59,845 --> 00:23:02,280
I was kind of traumatized by the event,

606
00:23:02,347 --> 00:23:05,450
so I stopped grilling meat alone at home.

607
00:23:05,517 --> 00:23:07,719
-That's an unfortunate memory.
-It is.

608
00:23:07,786 --> 00:23:09,588
So he rarely grills meat at home.

609
00:23:09,654 --> 00:23:12,791
You could've gone to your parents' place.
Why were you alone on a holiday?

610
00:23:12,858 --> 00:23:14,993
I usually stop by the day before.

611
00:23:15,060 --> 00:23:17,195
They'll start nagging
if I stay for too long.

612
00:23:17,262 --> 00:23:18,463
-Nagging.
-I return quickly.

613
00:23:18,530 --> 00:23:20,899
-Of course.
-Urging him to marry soon.

614
00:23:20,966 --> 00:23:23,034
Yes, that happens.

615
00:23:23,101 --> 00:23:24,903
Let's see the shelf-stable ingredients.

616
00:23:24,970 --> 00:23:26,505
They brought all that? Whoa.

617
00:23:26,571 --> 00:23:28,106
What are these noodles?

618
00:23:28,173 --> 00:23:31,009
It's probably instant ramen

619
00:23:31,076 --> 00:23:31,977
packaged like that.

620
00:23:32,043 --> 00:23:34,546
-You don't know what many of these are.
-No.

621
00:23:34,613 --> 00:23:37,249
-Some of them are from my fans.
-Gifts from fans.

622
00:23:37,315 --> 00:23:39,184
I'm curious about a few of those,

623
00:23:39,251 --> 00:23:41,920
so I wanted to ask the chefs
to use them today.

624
00:23:41,987 --> 00:23:43,722
-What is it?
-There's something

625
00:23:43,789 --> 00:23:45,290
similar to vinegar somewhere.

626
00:23:45,357 --> 00:23:46,491
It's balsamic vinegar.

627
00:23:46,558 --> 00:23:49,094
I got it as a present
and have never used it.

628
00:23:49,161 --> 00:23:51,229
-It looks good.
-That's a good one.

629
00:23:51,296 --> 00:23:52,397
Can I have it?

630
00:23:52,464 --> 00:23:53,532
GIGGLING

631
00:23:53,598 --> 00:23:55,667
-It'll taste good in a dish.
-Give it to me.

632
00:23:55,734 --> 00:23:56,968
I LOVE VINEGAR♥

633
00:23:57,035 --> 00:23:59,538
That soy sauce back there
is something I got

634
00:23:59,604 --> 00:24:02,007
while traveling in Japan
after hearing it was famous.

635
00:24:02,073 --> 00:24:03,909
It was really good,
so I want to eat something

636
00:24:03,975 --> 00:24:05,811
-that uses that soy sauce.
-Sure.

637
00:24:05,877 --> 00:24:07,679
-All these.
-These.

638
00:24:07,746 --> 00:24:09,648
-Please make use of these.
-These.

639
00:24:09,714 --> 00:24:11,950
That's all for Seok-jin's fridge.

640
00:24:12,017 --> 00:24:14,152
FINISHED CHECKING OUT SEOK-JIN'S FRIDGE

641
00:24:14,219 --> 00:24:17,255
I wonder what he's looking to eat today.

642
00:24:17,322 --> 00:24:19,958
Here are his orders. Show us.

643
00:24:21,960 --> 00:24:23,728
-Grown Man's Soup.
-Grown Man's Soup.

644
00:24:23,995 --> 00:24:25,297
PLEASED

645
00:24:25,363 --> 00:24:26,865
The old dude's soup.

646
00:24:26,932 --> 00:24:28,133
I get it already.

647
00:24:28,200 --> 00:24:29,935
-Gosh.
-That soup.

648
00:24:30,001 --> 00:24:30,969
Man.

649
00:24:31,036 --> 00:24:32,270
Something hearty.

650
00:24:32,337 --> 00:24:33,572
WHAT'S ALL THIS SOUND?

651
00:24:33,638 --> 00:24:37,175
I want to eat a soup dish
that makes me make those noises,

652
00:24:37,242 --> 00:24:38,977
made by professional chefs.

653
00:24:39,044 --> 00:24:40,445
Soup dish.

654
00:24:40,512 --> 00:24:41,913
Grown Man's Soup.

655
00:24:41,980 --> 00:24:43,715
What's the second order?

656
00:24:43,782 --> 00:24:45,917
-What's next? Problematic Meat.
-Problematic Meat.

657
00:24:46,852 --> 00:24:48,687
He has lots of meat. That's why.

658
00:24:48,753 --> 00:24:50,655
They're good meat.

659
00:24:50,722 --> 00:24:53,725
Both the beef and the pork
come in a variety of cuts too.

660
00:24:53,792 --> 00:24:55,794
I've never been able to use them,

661
00:24:55,861 --> 00:24:59,431
so I'd like to eat it
in a deliciously prepared dish.

662
00:24:59,498 --> 00:25:04,402
The four remaining chefs are Hyun-suk,
Sam, Eun-yeong, and Sung-jun.

663
00:25:04,469 --> 00:25:05,570
I'll go

664
00:25:05,637 --> 00:25:07,305
for Grown Man's Soup.

665
00:25:07,372 --> 00:25:10,642
-The soup?
-I like to eat like an old dude too, and…

666
00:25:10,709 --> 00:25:12,177
-And you have a beard.
-She's really bad.

667
00:25:12,244 --> 00:25:13,345
Yes, a beard.

668
00:25:13,411 --> 00:25:16,348
If you look closely, I have hair
on my chin. I'm an actual old dude.

669
00:25:16,414 --> 00:25:18,083
SUDDEN CONFESSION OF FACIAL HAIR

670
00:25:18,149 --> 00:25:21,086
-Seok-jin… Yes, you're right.
-It grew out already.

671
00:25:21,152 --> 00:25:22,621
You can see it by the evening.

672
00:25:22,687 --> 00:25:24,155
WHAT?

673
00:25:24,222 --> 00:25:25,590
She means facial hair.

674
00:25:26,191 --> 00:25:27,225
I didn't know what she meant.

675
00:25:27,292 --> 00:25:28,159
FACEPALM

676
00:25:28,226 --> 00:25:30,996
After I shared this,
someone sent me a shaver as a present.

677
00:25:31,563 --> 00:25:33,632
PROTECT THE GODDESS OF CHINESE CUISINE
FROM THE FANS

678
00:25:33,698 --> 00:25:36,368
-I'm serious.
-You can't show it on TV.

679
00:25:36,434 --> 00:25:39,638
-I really got it as a present.
-The high beams.

680
00:25:39,704 --> 00:25:41,673
Anyway, I want to make
a delicious Chinese dish.

681
00:25:41,740 --> 00:25:43,375
-Of course.
-I'll make soup.

682
00:25:43,441 --> 00:25:44,276
ALL RIGHT

683
00:25:45,143 --> 00:25:47,412
So Eun-yeong is in.

684
00:25:47,479 --> 00:25:49,481
We need someone older
to make that soup dish.

685
00:25:49,548 --> 00:25:53,084
-Hyun-suk.
-Hyun-suk should join for the grown man.

686
00:25:53,151 --> 00:25:54,386
I'll go.

687
00:25:54,452 --> 00:25:57,055
-Hyun-suk.
-I was going to go.

688
00:25:57,122 --> 00:26:00,125
-I have lots of ramen places pinned.
-He loves ramen too.

689
00:26:00,191 --> 00:26:01,960
I also love ramen so much,

690
00:26:02,027 --> 00:26:03,962
and I recommend great places

691
00:26:04,029 --> 00:26:05,931
-to my fellow chefs.
-He does.

692
00:26:05,997 --> 00:26:07,866
-He loves ramen.
-So I want to make him

693
00:26:07,933 --> 00:26:10,402
a bowl of ramen
that a grown man would love.

694
00:26:10,468 --> 00:26:11,303
Excuse me.

695
00:26:11,369 --> 00:26:13,672
The order isn't Grown Man's Tears.

696
00:26:14,506 --> 00:26:16,207
It's not tears, but soup.

697
00:26:16,274 --> 00:26:19,077
-It's so good that it'll make him cry.
-So delicious.

698
00:26:19,144 --> 00:26:21,546
Eun-yeong and Hyun-suk
are up against each other.

699
00:26:21,613 --> 00:26:24,482
-Then it's decided. Problematic Meat.
-Problematic Meat.

700
00:26:24,549 --> 00:26:27,519
-Sam versus Sung-jun.
-Sung-jun.

701
00:26:27,586 --> 00:26:30,322
Then let us begin the soup dish round

702
00:26:30,388 --> 00:26:32,557
between Eun-yeong and Hyun-suk!

703
00:26:34,726 --> 00:26:38,930
<i>Let us begin the first round of cook-off</i>
<i>with Seok-jin's ingredients.</i>

704
00:26:38,997 --> 00:26:41,566
The one who recently got
a shaver as a present,

705
00:26:41,633 --> 00:26:44,703
the self-proclaimed biggest old dude,
Chef Park Eun-yeong,

706
00:26:46,605 --> 00:26:51,943
versus the actual oldest dude
of the show, Chef Choi Hyun-suk.

707
00:26:52,010 --> 00:26:54,179
Did you shave your face? It looks clean.

708
00:26:54,245 --> 00:26:55,780
Some time has passed already,

709
00:26:55,847 --> 00:26:58,149
so I should go and shave again
around 6 to 7 p.m.

710
00:26:59,250 --> 00:27:00,986
-But you did it in the morning.
-I did.

711
00:27:01,052 --> 00:27:02,988
-And again at 7 p.m.
-Twice a day?

712
00:27:03,054 --> 00:27:05,690
She'd throw anything out there
if it could help her win stars.

713
00:27:05,757 --> 00:27:07,325
-I go feral for the stars.
-What now?

714
00:27:07,392 --> 00:27:08,693
I go feral for the stars.

715
00:27:08,760 --> 00:27:10,495
HOW CUTE

716
00:27:10,562 --> 00:27:12,497
Her charm is that you can never hate her.

717
00:27:12,897 --> 00:27:14,499
SMILE

718
00:27:14,566 --> 00:27:16,301
-Look at her face.
-She's bubbly.

719
00:27:16,368 --> 00:27:19,104
It looks like
something else is on her mind.

720
00:27:19,170 --> 00:27:21,973
No one can read her face.

721
00:27:22,040 --> 00:27:25,710
Eun-yeong, do you believe
it's disadvantageous for Hyun-suk?

722
00:27:25,777 --> 00:27:27,612
Yes, it's because

723
00:27:27,679 --> 00:27:29,681
the order is Grown Man's Soup.

724
00:27:29,748 --> 00:27:31,516
Chef Hyun-suk isn't a grown man,

725
00:27:31,583 --> 00:27:33,184
but an aged man.

726
00:27:33,818 --> 00:27:35,820
He doesn't suit the topic.

727
00:27:35,887 --> 00:27:38,757
He hears something like this,
but he still laughs it off.

728
00:27:38,823 --> 00:27:40,825
-What if it were me?
-It would've been awful.

729
00:27:40,892 --> 00:27:42,627
He'd wushu me right away.

730
00:27:42,694 --> 00:27:44,496
-You tend to favor her.
-He does.

731
00:27:44,562 --> 00:27:45,430
I wouldn't call it that.

732
00:27:45,497 --> 00:27:47,499
-Is it because he has a daughter?
-Well…

733
00:27:47,565 --> 00:27:48,767
She always tries hard

734
00:27:48,833 --> 00:27:50,969
-and stays bubbly in her demeanor.
-Yes.

735
00:27:51,036 --> 00:27:53,672
I think that's great,
so I want to show support.

736
00:27:53,738 --> 00:27:55,573
-I do the same.
-Is Sung-jun different?

737
00:27:55,640 --> 00:27:57,308
-He tries hard too.
-He always does his best.

738
00:27:57,375 --> 00:27:58,743
And I'm bubbly too.

739
00:27:58,810 --> 00:28:02,113
Turning on the emergency light
at the right time is not an easy job.

740
00:28:02,180 --> 00:28:03,081
You're right.

741
00:28:03,148 --> 00:28:05,717
-He plays a big part.
-He's in charge of the "emergency" meme.

742
00:28:05,784 --> 00:28:07,452
Well, everyone tries hard.

743
00:28:07,519 --> 00:28:08,887
He'll do well on his own.

744
00:28:09,220 --> 00:28:10,522
MEANING: HE DOESN'T NEED ME

745
00:28:10,588 --> 00:28:11,723
He just doesn't like him.

746
00:28:11,790 --> 00:28:14,059
-What do you mean?
-You're on your own.

747
00:28:14,125 --> 00:28:16,394
She also said something like this.

748
00:28:16,461 --> 00:28:19,864
"Hyun-suk isn't exactly a <i>ggondae</i>,
a nosy boomer."

749
00:28:19,931 --> 00:28:21,966
-What do you mean?
-He's not a <i>ggondae?</i>

750
00:28:22,033 --> 00:28:23,968
In my opinion,
for someone to be a <i>ggondae,</i>

751
00:28:24,035 --> 00:28:26,037
they'll have to be nosy.

752
00:28:26,104 --> 00:28:28,973
-But he doesn't care about anyone else.
-Right.

753
00:28:29,040 --> 00:28:30,408
So he's not a <i>ggondae.</i>

754
00:28:30,608 --> 00:28:31,843
IS IT A COMPLIMENT OR WHAT?

755
00:28:31,910 --> 00:28:34,379
I share my MBTI type with him weekly,
and he forgets every time.

756
00:28:35,814 --> 00:28:37,082
And he's surprised each time?

757
00:28:37,148 --> 00:28:38,049
Every single time.

758
00:28:38,116 --> 00:28:39,984
He always goes, "The same as me?"

759
00:28:40,051 --> 00:28:41,419
He only cares about his business.

760
00:28:41,486 --> 00:28:44,022
-That's hilarious.
-So what's Sung-jun's MBTI type?

761
00:28:44,089 --> 00:28:46,391
His MBTI type is

762
00:28:46,458 --> 00:28:47,659
ENTJ.

763
00:28:47,726 --> 00:28:49,961
-We just said it was the same as his.
-I told him today.

764
00:28:50,028 --> 00:28:51,429
I shared it with him today,

765
00:28:51,496 --> 00:28:53,865
and he tends to dislike
people who are like him.

766
00:28:53,932 --> 00:28:55,600
He definitely does.

767
00:28:55,667 --> 00:28:58,002
-Like he's looking in a mirror.
-It should be his therapy.

768
00:28:58,069 --> 00:29:00,371
We tend to dislike
those who are the most like us.

769
00:29:00,438 --> 00:29:01,406
CLENCHING TEETH…

770
00:29:01,473 --> 00:29:02,741
That's why he's my master.

771
00:29:03,575 --> 00:29:05,610
CHEF EUN-YEONG'S INGREDIENTS:
DRIED DOKDO SHRIMP, HEADLESS DRIED SHRIMP

772
00:29:05,677 --> 00:29:07,712
COCKTAIL SHRIMP, TILEFISH,
KNIFE-CUT NOODLES, AND MORE

773
00:29:07,779 --> 00:29:10,815
First up, Chef Eun-yeong.
What are you making?

774
00:29:10,882 --> 00:29:14,219
In order to attack the aged man,
Hyun-suk the OCC, OldCreatorCrew,

775
00:29:14,285 --> 00:29:15,587
I prepared a dish called

776
00:29:15,653 --> 00:29:16,654
OldSoupSoup.

777
00:29:16,721 --> 00:29:17,956
OldSoupSoup?

778
00:29:18,022 --> 00:29:20,992
-OldSoupSoup.
-OldSoupSoup. Not OCC. OSS.

779
00:29:21,059 --> 00:29:22,894
-OldSoupSoup.
-I see.

780
00:29:22,961 --> 00:29:25,463
It's an attack on Hyun-suk.

781
00:29:25,530 --> 00:29:28,066
I'm going to make
Hong Kong-style shrimp noodle soup.

782
00:29:29,467 --> 00:29:32,070
Shrimp noodle soup
is usually made with fresh shrimp,

783
00:29:32,137 --> 00:29:34,205
but he didn't have any.

784
00:29:34,272 --> 00:29:36,808
So I'll use dried shrimp for the broth,

785
00:29:36,875 --> 00:29:41,546
and I'll also add shrimp that'll seem
freshly caught, although they weren't.

786
00:29:41,613 --> 00:29:44,048
-With frozen shrimp?
-I'll make the noodle soup

787
00:29:44,115 --> 00:29:46,451
that he'll love and start chugging on.

788
00:29:46,518 --> 00:29:48,052
That's what I've prepared.

789
00:29:48,119 --> 00:29:50,989
-All right. That sounds good.
-It sounds so good.

790
00:29:51,923 --> 00:29:53,925
CHEF HYUN-SUK'S INGREDIENTS:
DRIED DOKDO SHRIMP, HEADLESS DRIED SHRIMP

791
00:29:53,992 --> 00:29:55,994
PORK BELLY, SCALLION, KELP SEASONING,
SOY SAUCE, BEEF BONE BROTH

792
00:29:56,060 --> 00:29:58,696
The name of my dish is

793
00:29:58,763 --> 00:29:59,931
Don't Shrimp Me

794
00:30:00,498 --> 00:30:01,599
Because I'm Old.

795
00:30:02,867 --> 00:30:04,435
A chef never ages.

796
00:30:04,502 --> 00:30:05,970
He merely gets more ripe with experience.

797
00:30:07,205 --> 00:30:09,207
-Overcooked.
-Completely cooked.

798
00:30:09,274 --> 00:30:11,276
He's like well-aged kimchi.

799
00:30:11,342 --> 00:30:13,511
-Way past cooked.
-Well-done.

800
00:30:14,145 --> 00:30:15,313
He's glaring at you.

801
00:30:15,380 --> 00:30:17,949
-He glares at you when you talk.
-Why do you do that?

802
00:30:18,016 --> 00:30:20,151
-He always glares at you.
-My favorite ramen joint

803
00:30:20,218 --> 00:30:23,054
is a place in Hokkaido

804
00:30:23,121 --> 00:30:24,589
famous for shrimp ramen.

805
00:30:25,323 --> 00:30:28,760
You have to wait an hour and a half
even at midnight.

806
00:30:28,827 --> 00:30:32,831
And when you're nearby, the air smells
like broth made with 100,000 shrimps.

807
00:30:33,631 --> 00:30:35,066
It's my all-time favorite ramen,

808
00:30:35,133 --> 00:30:37,101
and it's extremely rich and savory.

809
00:30:37,168 --> 00:30:40,271
So I'd like to make
this heavily flavored ramen

810
00:30:40,338 --> 00:30:44,008
that old men can truly enjoy

811
00:30:44,075 --> 00:30:46,110
after facing years of hardships.

812
00:30:46,177 --> 00:30:49,647
The restaurant he mentioned
has such a long line

813
00:30:49,714 --> 00:30:51,983
-that I had given up.
-It's great.

814
00:30:52,050 --> 00:30:53,451
-It's that long?
-It's so long.

815
00:30:53,518 --> 00:30:55,987
-I haven't had it either.
-And you can try that today.

816
00:30:56,054 --> 00:30:56,888
It's really famous.

817
00:30:56,955 --> 00:30:59,324
-It's a shrimp round.
-Yes.

818
00:30:59,390 --> 00:31:00,592
Will you make the noodles?

819
00:31:00,658 --> 00:31:01,893
I'm making fresh noodles.

820
00:31:01,960 --> 00:31:03,895
WILL MAKE FRESH NOODLES

821
00:31:03,962 --> 00:31:07,932
And now, let us begin the round
"Grown Man's Soup."

822
00:31:08,800 --> 00:31:10,201
David and Goliath.

823
00:31:10,268 --> 00:31:13,838
She married solely for this round.

824
00:31:13,905 --> 00:31:17,675
She's determined to win a star
from her husband's lookalike,

825
00:31:17,742 --> 00:31:19,611
Chef Park Eun-yeong.

826
00:31:19,677 --> 00:31:23,781
He had aged solely for this round.

827
00:31:23,848 --> 00:31:26,517
-Our oldest OldCreatorCrew,
-OCC!

828
00:31:26,584 --> 00:31:28,419
…Chef Choi Hyun-suk.

829
00:31:28,486 --> 00:31:32,757
Who will capture the heart
of the grown man, Ha Seok-jin?

830
00:31:32,824 --> 00:31:35,827
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

831
00:31:36,928 --> 00:31:38,529
It's a soup dish round.

832
00:31:38,897 --> 00:31:40,531
A SOUP DISH ROUND!

833
00:31:40,932 --> 00:31:42,634
It's a soup dish round.

834
00:31:42,700 --> 00:31:44,969
He's toasting the shrimp.

835
00:31:45,036 --> 00:31:46,838
He starts by toasting the shrimp.

836
00:31:46,905 --> 00:31:47,872
HEADLESS DRIED SHRIMP

837
00:31:47,939 --> 00:31:50,241
Is it better to boil the broth
after toasting them?

838
00:31:50,308 --> 00:31:52,010
This brings out more flavor.

839
00:31:52,377 --> 00:31:53,211
YOU!

840
00:31:53,511 --> 00:31:55,146
TINY SHAKES

841
00:31:55,213 --> 00:31:57,715
-That's <i>mantecare.</i> "I'll do what you do."
-I don't do it like that.

842
00:31:57,982 --> 00:31:59,651
AN HOMAGE TO HIS OTHER SELF

843
00:31:59,717 --> 00:32:02,887
He toasts the shrimp
to use them as the broth.

844
00:32:04,088 --> 00:32:05,290
Whoa. Japanese kelp seasoning.

845
00:32:05,356 --> 00:32:07,358
He threw in kelp seasoning and pork belly.

846
00:32:07,425 --> 00:32:09,160
You're adding kelp into the soup?

847
00:32:10,395 --> 00:32:11,963
-With pork.
-Is it… What's that?

848
00:32:12,030 --> 00:32:13,031
-What did you squeeze in?
-Ginger?

849
00:32:13,097 --> 00:32:14,165
-Garlic?
-Ginger.

850
00:32:14,232 --> 00:32:15,500
Talk to us while you cook.

851
00:32:15,566 --> 00:32:16,668
What did you squeeze in?

852
00:32:16,734 --> 00:32:18,036
Hey, OCC!

853
00:32:18,102 --> 00:32:19,170
-Chef OCC!
-He can't hear you.

854
00:32:19,570 --> 00:32:21,739
THE INGREDIENT DETECTOR IS ON IT

855
00:32:21,806 --> 00:32:22,640
SNIFF

856
00:32:22,707 --> 00:32:23,908
-It's ginger.
-Ginger.

857
00:32:23,975 --> 00:32:25,877
I see. Thank you, Ho-young.

858
00:32:27,478 --> 00:32:29,280
WHAT'S EUN-YEONG'S FIRST STEP?

859
00:32:29,847 --> 00:32:31,416
She starts with garlic.

860
00:32:31,482 --> 00:32:33,384
ONE SHOT, ONE KILL

861
00:32:33,785 --> 00:32:34,886
PREPS THE INGREDIENTS FIRST!

862
00:32:35,286 --> 00:32:38,623
1. PREP GARLIC, GREEN ONION, AND TOMATOES

863
00:32:38,690 --> 00:32:39,857
FINISHED PREPPING, AND…

864
00:32:41,159 --> 00:32:43,728
As expected,
she's toasting the shrimp too.

865
00:32:44,662 --> 00:32:47,131
-You're right.
-She added tomatoes too.

866
00:32:47,198 --> 00:32:48,099
With tomatoes?

867
00:32:48,266 --> 00:32:49,100
STORE-BOUGHT VEGGIE BROTH

868
00:32:49,934 --> 00:32:53,471
I somehow thought
she'd make meat-based broth.

869
00:32:53,538 --> 00:32:55,540
-Spicy.
-What's that you just added?

870
00:32:55,606 --> 00:32:57,075
-Red pepper powder.
-A bit of red pepper powder.

871
00:32:57,141 --> 00:32:59,143
-Tomatoes, dried shrimp…
-Hong Kong-style.

872
00:33:00,211 --> 00:33:01,879
2 EGGS

873
00:33:01,946 --> 00:33:03,881
Is he cooking eggs?

874
00:33:04,315 --> 00:33:05,650
WHAT ABOUT THE TOASTED SHRIMP?

875
00:33:05,717 --> 00:33:07,285
Shrimp is the key ingredient for both.

876
00:33:07,352 --> 00:33:09,253
INTO THE BONE BROTH!

877
00:33:09,320 --> 00:33:11,222
It'll have so much umami.

878
00:33:11,889 --> 00:33:13,458
-What?
-<i>Ramyeon</i> noodles?

879
00:33:13,524 --> 00:33:14,859
-<i>Ramyeon?</i>
-To make it thicker.

880
00:33:14,926 --> 00:33:16,361
To add starch.

881
00:33:16,427 --> 00:33:18,396
-He wants to make it thicker.
-By adding starch.

882
00:33:18,463 --> 00:33:20,598
-I see.
-He's making it richer.

883
00:33:20,665 --> 00:33:24,002
The soup tastes much deeper
once you cook it with oil-fried noodles.

884
00:33:24,068 --> 00:33:25,570
And it adds richness.

885
00:33:25,636 --> 00:33:27,405
But the noodles will get soggy.

886
00:33:27,472 --> 00:33:28,639
He won't use it.

887
00:33:28,706 --> 00:33:30,208
-Just the starchiness.
-It's a great idea.

888
00:33:30,441 --> 00:33:32,577
WHAT'S NEXT?

889
00:33:32,643 --> 00:33:34,278
He's using that soy sauce too.

890
00:33:34,345 --> 00:33:36,781
He's adding soy sauce and cooking wine.

891
00:33:36,848 --> 00:33:40,084
I think he'll marinate
the boiled egg in the sauce.

892
00:33:41,886 --> 00:33:43,588
Eun-yeong uses tilefish.

893
00:33:43,654 --> 00:33:44,489
PREPPING THE FISH!

894
00:33:44,756 --> 00:33:46,524
3. SLICE TILEFISH FILLETS

895
00:33:46,591 --> 00:33:48,960
Is that for the shrimp soup?

896
00:33:49,027 --> 00:33:50,428
I'm making fish balls.

897
00:33:50,495 --> 00:33:52,163
Fish balls. You're Meatball Queen.

898
00:33:52,230 --> 00:33:53,431
He didn't have fresh shrimp.

899
00:33:53,664 --> 00:33:55,433
FISH BALLS FOR THE SHRIMP NOODLE SOUP!

900
00:33:55,500 --> 00:33:57,168
Are you only using the meat?

901
00:33:57,235 --> 00:33:58,436
Yes, just the meat.

902
00:33:58,870 --> 00:33:59,704
TILEFISH MEAT

903
00:34:00,371 --> 00:34:01,973
I guess she'll grind it.

904
00:34:02,040 --> 00:34:05,309
-Yes.
-She'll grind it to make fish balls.

905
00:34:05,376 --> 00:34:07,912
-She's making fish balls right now.
-She's only using egg whites.

906
00:34:07,979 --> 00:34:09,814
Why do you only use egg whites?

907
00:34:09,881 --> 00:34:11,315
If you use whole eggs,

908
00:34:11,382 --> 00:34:13,451
the yolk creates a dull, sticky flavor.

909
00:34:13,518 --> 00:34:16,687
So I'm using just the egg whites as glue.

910
00:34:16,754 --> 00:34:19,390
She's making fish balls with tilefish.

911
00:34:19,457 --> 00:34:20,425
SESAME OIL

912
00:34:20,491 --> 00:34:23,394
-You need fish balls for the noodle soup.
-Of course.

913
00:34:23,461 --> 00:34:25,797
It adds a nice touch.

914
00:34:25,863 --> 00:34:28,399
The fish balls. The meatball queen.

915
00:34:28,466 --> 00:34:29,934
She's making fish balls.

916
00:34:30,001 --> 00:34:32,170
-Fish balls.
-Fish balls.

917
00:34:32,236 --> 00:34:34,072
She made one already.

918
00:34:34,138 --> 00:34:35,673
She's very fast.

919
00:34:35,740 --> 00:34:36,674
"Boing."

920
00:34:37,942 --> 00:34:39,710
-Fish balls.
-Like shrimp balls.

921
00:34:39,777 --> 00:34:41,245
-"Boing."
-She made it so easily.

922
00:34:41,579 --> 00:34:43,881
FISH BALLS ARE MADE INSTANTLY

923
00:34:44,682 --> 00:34:45,583
FLOUR

924
00:34:45,650 --> 00:34:47,985
2. MAKE NOODLE DOUGH
BY MIXING FLOUR AND WATER

925
00:34:48,052 --> 00:34:48,920
The dough.

926
00:34:50,388 --> 00:34:52,190
It looks a bit too thin.

927
00:34:53,458 --> 00:34:55,359
QUICKLY ADDS MORE FLOUR

928
00:34:55,426 --> 00:34:56,494
Trouble with the noodles.

929
00:34:56,561 --> 00:34:58,496
-There's trouble?
-It's not working?

930
00:34:58,563 --> 00:35:00,164
GRINDS AGAIN

931
00:35:00,231 --> 00:35:01,666
Let's see if it turned out okay.

932
00:35:01,732 --> 00:35:03,267
It looks good. Nice dough.

933
00:35:03,334 --> 00:35:04,235
DO NOT WORRY!

934
00:35:04,969 --> 00:35:06,237
OLDCREATORCREW!

935
00:35:07,071 --> 00:35:08,539
SHIELDING WITH HIS WHOLE BODY

936
00:35:09,140 --> 00:35:10,274
OH, BOY

937
00:35:10,341 --> 00:35:11,576
For ramen.

938
00:35:11,642 --> 00:35:13,044
He's making the noodles.

939
00:35:13,111 --> 00:35:14,745
First, flatten the dough.

940
00:35:14,812 --> 00:35:16,180
One more time.

941
00:35:16,247 --> 00:35:18,116
Jong-won isn't allowed to help.

942
00:35:18,182 --> 00:35:19,417
GIGGLE

943
00:35:19,484 --> 00:35:22,353
-We have to see it.
-There was a controversy.

944
00:35:23,421 --> 00:35:25,957
<i>It's nice that Jong-won is helping,</i>
<i>but his head is blocking the view.</i>

945
00:35:26,023 --> 00:35:27,692
A CONTROVERSY OVER THE CAMERA BLOCK

946
00:35:27,959 --> 00:35:29,327
WHAT ABOUT THIS TIME?

947
00:35:29,393 --> 00:35:31,963
It looks like his noodles
have turned out thin and nice.

948
00:35:32,196 --> 00:35:33,164
SMOOTH

949
00:35:33,231 --> 00:35:35,466
That's Hyun-suk.

950
00:35:35,533 --> 00:35:36,601
It's taking him a while.

951
00:35:36,667 --> 00:35:38,202
SNICKERING…

952
00:35:38,269 --> 00:35:41,005
You're about five minutes in.

953
00:35:41,072 --> 00:35:42,540
THAT PUNK…

954
00:35:42,840 --> 00:35:44,075
THE DOUGH IS READY

955
00:35:44,142 --> 00:35:44,976
So funny.

956
00:35:45,176 --> 00:35:46,544
WHAT'S EUN-YEONG UP TO?

957
00:35:47,645 --> 00:35:49,180
Was it just egg whites? Or starch too?

958
00:35:49,247 --> 00:35:50,882
I added a bit of starch too.

959
00:35:50,948 --> 00:35:52,683
She made that with tilefish, right?

960
00:35:52,750 --> 00:35:54,118
-Tilefish?
-I did.

961
00:35:54,185 --> 00:35:55,019
STICK THE TAIL ON

962
00:35:55,453 --> 00:35:56,787
AND THE HEAD

963
00:35:57,922 --> 00:35:59,924
A SHRIMP-SHAPED FISH BALL

964
00:36:01,526 --> 00:36:04,695
He didn't have fresh shrimp,
so I wanted to give him fish balls.

965
00:36:04,762 --> 00:36:05,897
Fish balls.

966
00:36:05,963 --> 00:36:08,166
He's making the ramen noodles now.

967
00:36:08,733 --> 00:36:10,668
He's making the noodles now.

968
00:36:10,735 --> 00:36:11,602
It's coming out.

969
00:36:11,669 --> 00:36:13,037
In tagliatelle size.

970
00:36:13,871 --> 00:36:14,839
COMES OUT SMOOTHLY

971
00:36:14,906 --> 00:36:16,440
-They're thick.
-We can see it now.

972
00:36:16,507 --> 00:36:18,643
He just made fresh noodles.

973
00:36:18,709 --> 00:36:20,178
Aren't they a bit too thick?

974
00:36:20,244 --> 00:36:22,246
-They look thick.
-They're pretty thick for ramen.

975
00:36:22,313 --> 00:36:23,347
-Right?
-How unique.

976
00:36:23,414 --> 00:36:25,283
It looks like <i>kal-guksu</i> noodles.

977
00:36:25,349 --> 00:36:27,151
In that case, shouldn't you have asked him

978
00:36:27,218 --> 00:36:28,953
his preferred thickness of the noodles?

979
00:36:29,020 --> 00:36:30,021
-Yes.
-Right?

980
00:36:30,087 --> 00:36:32,790
Do you prefer
thin noodles or thick noodles?

981
00:36:32,857 --> 00:36:34,358
I actually prefer

982
00:36:34,425 --> 00:36:36,060
-thin noodles.
-Thin noodles!

983
00:36:36,127 --> 00:36:37,028
OH, NO!

984
00:36:37,094 --> 00:36:37,929
No!

985
00:36:37,995 --> 00:36:39,263
Thin noodles.

986
00:36:39,330 --> 00:36:40,565
Thin noodles!

987
00:36:41,132 --> 00:36:42,166
You're done.

988
00:36:42,500 --> 00:36:43,768
…THAT WAS SO HARSH…

989
00:36:43,834 --> 00:36:45,436
Must he just eat what he's given?

990
00:36:45,503 --> 00:36:46,804
-What now?
-Chef's choice.

991
00:36:46,871 --> 00:36:47,939
He's unable to choose.

992
00:36:48,005 --> 00:36:49,507
It was chef's choice.

993
00:36:49,574 --> 00:36:51,943
-He has to eat what he's given.
-You're right.

994
00:36:53,344 --> 00:36:54,812
Why are you glaring at me?

995
00:36:54,879 --> 00:36:56,247
I didn't say I didn't like it.

996
00:36:56,514 --> 00:36:57,715
THE FRIDGE OWNER DOESN'T

997
00:36:58,316 --> 00:36:59,150
CHECKS FOR TIME

998
00:37:00,484 --> 00:37:01,519
He's kneading it again?

999
00:37:01,586 --> 00:37:03,588
-Wait, really?
-Are you serious?

1000
00:37:03,654 --> 00:37:05,389
-Don't do it.
-It's okay.

1001
00:37:05,456 --> 00:37:06,724
-You don't have time.
-Come on.

1002
00:37:06,791 --> 00:37:09,660
-Don't do it.
-He's determined. And mad.

1003
00:37:09,727 --> 00:37:10,761
You're making it again?

1004
00:37:10,828 --> 00:37:12,930
-He's making thin noodles now.
-You're doing it over?

1005
00:37:12,997 --> 00:37:14,498
The OCC is mad.

1006
00:37:14,565 --> 00:37:16,234
-Really?
-The OCC is mad.

1007
00:37:16,968 --> 00:37:19,503
-The grandpa never yields.
-Yes, he's stubborn.

1008
00:37:19,570 --> 00:37:20,771
The stubborn grandpa.

1009
00:37:20,838 --> 00:37:22,073
It has too much flour.

1010
00:37:22,139 --> 00:37:24,942
-Can he fix that right away?
-No time, but he tries again.

1011
00:37:25,009 --> 00:37:27,144
-Wait, why?
-Are you serious?

1012
00:37:27,211 --> 00:37:28,479
Eight minutes left.

1013
00:37:28,546 --> 00:37:31,182
I mean, Seok-jin said
he liked thin noodles.

1014
00:37:31,249 --> 00:37:32,984
-We didn't say it.
-True.

1015
00:37:33,050 --> 00:37:34,485
IF THAT'S WHAT THE GUEST WANTS!

1016
00:37:34,652 --> 00:37:36,254
PUSHING

1017
00:37:36,320 --> 00:37:37,521
Made to order.

1018
00:37:43,094 --> 00:37:44,495
If he succeeds, it'll be huge.

1019
00:37:44,562 --> 00:37:46,264
He looks mad.

1020
00:37:46,330 --> 00:37:47,865
-He seems angry.
-You know what?

1021
00:37:47,932 --> 00:37:50,701
I guess the noodles will be quite chewy.

1022
00:37:50,768 --> 00:37:51,969
You've kneaded it a lot.

1023
00:37:52,036 --> 00:37:53,838
Yes, the gluten must be in action.

1024
00:37:53,904 --> 00:37:56,340
He's making thinner noodles.

1025
00:37:56,407 --> 00:37:58,542
It'd be great if someone could hold this.

1026
00:37:58,609 --> 00:37:59,910
He wants someone to hold it.

1027
00:38:00,911 --> 00:38:02,580
-Is it ready? It is.
-They're thin.

1028
00:38:03,748 --> 00:38:04,882
AS THIN AS THREAD

1029
00:38:04,949 --> 00:38:06,017
Is that what you wanted?

1030
00:38:06,083 --> 00:38:08,052
-Yes, it looks perfect.
-He says yes.

1031
00:38:08,119 --> 00:38:10,488
That's more like it. He actually did it.

1032
00:38:10,554 --> 00:38:11,822
That's what he wanted.

1033
00:38:11,889 --> 00:38:13,257
Yes, that's what I'm talking about.

1034
00:38:15,326 --> 00:38:17,028
DON'T TEASE ME BECAUSE I'M OLD

1035
00:38:17,261 --> 00:38:18,863
WE WON'T♥

1036
00:38:18,929 --> 00:38:19,930
That was so cool.

1037
00:38:21,032 --> 00:38:22,733
That's what old dudes are like.

1038
00:38:22,800 --> 00:38:24,435
They may grumble, but they'll do it.

1039
00:38:24,502 --> 00:38:26,070
<i>-Yes.</i>
<i>-Yes.</i>

1040
00:38:26,137 --> 00:38:27,705
<i>He's like, "Is this what you want?"</i>

1041
00:38:27,772 --> 00:38:28,673
He's such a grown man.

1042
00:38:28,739 --> 00:38:30,841
Excuse me.
Aren't the noodles a bit too thin?

1043
00:38:30,908 --> 00:38:32,743
No, those are fine.

1044
00:38:32,810 --> 00:38:34,712
-Make them thicker.
-Those are way too thin.

1045
00:38:34,779 --> 00:38:36,881
-Should I stop? Aren't they too thin?
-Yes.

1046
00:38:36,947 --> 00:38:37,782
They seem too thin.

1047
00:38:37,848 --> 00:38:39,917
-He wants something in the middle.
-They're too thin.

1048
00:38:39,984 --> 00:38:41,852
-No.
-You make it yourself, then!

1049
00:38:42,053 --> 00:38:43,688
THE OLD DUDE'S ANGRY…

1050
00:38:43,921 --> 00:38:49,427
4. CUT THE BOILED EGGS IN HALF
AND MARINATE IN SEASONED SOY SAUCE

1051
00:38:49,627 --> 00:38:51,395
WHAT'S EUN-YEONG UP TO?

1052
00:38:51,629 --> 00:38:52,930
PULLING OUT THE SHRIMP BALLS!

1053
00:38:52,997 --> 00:38:54,865
BOILED, DEEP-FRIED

1054
00:38:55,066 --> 00:38:56,534
KNIFE-CUT NOODLES

1055
00:38:56,767 --> 00:38:58,469
BEGINS TO COOK THE NOODLES

1056
00:38:58,536 --> 00:39:00,304
She put the noodles in already.

1057
00:39:00,571 --> 00:39:01,906
WHAT ABOUT THE SHRIMP BROTH?

1058
00:39:02,606 --> 00:39:04,308
BLENDING EVERYTHING IN A BLENDER

1059
00:39:04,642 --> 00:39:06,477
Is that the soup, Eun-yeong?

1060
00:39:06,544 --> 00:39:08,245
-It's reddish.
-The soup's ready?

1061
00:39:08,312 --> 00:39:09,914
Yes, it's the broth.

1062
00:39:09,980 --> 00:39:11,215
She's really fast.

1063
00:39:11,282 --> 00:39:14,652
She finished making the soup
while Hyun-suk was acting stubborn.

1064
00:39:14,719 --> 00:39:16,053
I just blended the soup for richness

1065
00:39:16,120 --> 00:39:17,621
-to save time.
-Right. Tomatoes.

1066
00:39:17,688 --> 00:39:19,924
-She added tomatoes.
-She did.

1067
00:39:19,990 --> 00:39:21,892
And some more seasoning. Oyster sauce.

1068
00:39:21,959 --> 00:39:23,728
That soup looks so good.

1069
00:39:23,794 --> 00:39:25,229
You don't have much time.

1070
00:39:25,296 --> 00:39:28,432
You have 5 minutes and 30 seconds,
but I don't see the soup.

1071
00:39:28,499 --> 00:39:29,734
Scallions.

1072
00:39:30,835 --> 00:39:32,269
The noodles are too thin.

1073
00:39:32,336 --> 00:39:34,004
HE'LL END UP TAKING A BLOW SOON…

1074
00:39:34,071 --> 00:39:35,272
He has to cook them too.

1075
00:39:35,339 --> 00:39:37,408
-Those noodles…
-They only take three minutes, though.

1076
00:39:37,475 --> 00:39:38,943
…don't take much to cook.

1077
00:39:39,009 --> 00:39:41,445
Sung-ju, why don't you go to the kitchen

1078
00:39:41,512 --> 00:39:42,947
and talk about the noodles some more?

1079
00:39:43,013 --> 00:39:43,848
All right.

1080
00:39:43,914 --> 00:39:45,216
Let me go check.

1081
00:39:45,282 --> 00:39:46,984
I'm looking forward to these dishes.

1082
00:39:47,051 --> 00:39:49,120
Hyun-suk's soup isn't ready yet.

1083
00:39:49,186 --> 00:39:51,589
Eun-yeong's soup is ready
with some tomatoes.

1084
00:39:51,655 --> 00:39:53,858
This soup is everything
for this soup round.

1085
00:39:53,924 --> 00:39:56,127
The soup for a grown man,
made by Eun-yeong.

1086
00:39:56,193 --> 00:39:58,896
Is it OldSoupSoup?

1087
00:39:58,963 --> 00:40:01,665
Is it OldSoupSoup?

1088
00:40:01,732 --> 00:40:03,000
It's a bit spicy…

1089
00:40:03,067 --> 00:40:03,901
Huh?

1090
00:40:04,201 --> 00:40:05,669
WHAT IS IT?

1091
00:40:05,736 --> 00:40:07,138
It can use a little more kick.

1092
00:40:07,204 --> 00:40:08,839
-I'll add Cheongyang pepper.
-You will?

1093
00:40:08,906 --> 00:40:09,774
Yes.

1094
00:40:09,840 --> 00:40:11,742
What's with that facial expression?

1095
00:40:11,809 --> 00:40:13,644
Because old dudes love spicy food.

1096
00:40:14,011 --> 00:40:15,246
THE CHEEKY GIRL YOU CAN'T HATE

1097
00:40:15,312 --> 00:40:17,348
Gosh, I felt bad for asking.

1098
00:40:17,415 --> 00:40:20,751
Anyway, it still lacks
some depth in the flavor.

1099
00:40:20,818 --> 00:40:22,286
-Not enough depth.
-I still have time.

1100
00:40:22,486 --> 00:40:23,788
TOMATO SAUCE

1101
00:40:23,854 --> 00:40:24,688
CHEONGYANG PEPPER

1102
00:40:24,989 --> 00:40:27,825
ADDING UMAMI AND A SPICY KICK

1103
00:40:28,192 --> 00:40:29,059
IS HE OPENING IT?

1104
00:40:29,126 --> 00:40:30,127
Can you open it?

1105
00:40:30,194 --> 00:40:32,296
-Let me try the soup.
-It opens?

1106
00:40:32,363 --> 00:40:33,697
How did it happen right away?

1107
00:40:33,764 --> 00:40:35,733
It's a low-pressure cooker.

1108
00:40:35,800 --> 00:40:37,835
Something's out. What's this?

1109
00:40:37,902 --> 00:40:39,737
Hyun-suk's soup is ready too.

1110
00:40:39,804 --> 00:40:41,305
Whoa. In a pressure cooker?

1111
00:40:41,372 --> 00:40:44,408
-There are lots of ingredients in here.
-That's a lot.

1112
00:40:44,475 --> 00:40:46,043
That's pork.

1113
00:40:46,110 --> 00:40:48,145
Throw it in. Yes, throw it inside.

1114
00:40:48,212 --> 00:40:50,448
<i>Chashu.</i> Pork slices.

1115
00:40:51,449 --> 00:40:53,751
-It already looks so good.
-Look at that.

1116
00:40:53,818 --> 00:40:55,219
The noodles.

1117
00:40:55,286 --> 00:40:58,189
You need to cook the noodles.
You have three minutes left now.

1118
00:40:58,255 --> 00:41:01,358
-Hyun-suk cooks the noodles.
-They might stick to each other.

1119
00:41:01,425 --> 00:41:02,726
Let me try the soup.

1120
00:41:02,793 --> 00:41:04,728
He cooked it in a pressure cooker.

1121
00:41:04,795 --> 00:41:05,863
The soup.

1122
00:41:05,930 --> 00:41:07,198
SLURP

1123
00:41:07,264 --> 00:41:09,667
Does it taste like
you won't tease him for being old?

1124
00:41:10,334 --> 00:41:11,735
AMAZED!

1125
00:41:11,802 --> 00:41:14,905
It's so good!

1126
00:41:15,406 --> 00:41:17,007
REALLY?

1127
00:41:17,608 --> 00:41:19,243
That's what Hyun-suk does!

1128
00:41:19,310 --> 00:41:21,779
I love it, Hyun-suk!

1129
00:41:21,846 --> 00:41:23,614
-I love it!
-It's that good?

1130
00:41:23,681 --> 00:41:25,783
It's the kind of soup that old dudes love.

1131
00:41:25,983 --> 00:41:27,318
THE OLD DUDE GETS EXCITED

1132
00:41:27,384 --> 00:41:29,720
How did you add such depth to the soup?

1133
00:41:29,787 --> 00:41:32,156
You'll have to try
a little harder, Eun-yeong.

1134
00:41:32,223 --> 00:41:34,859
This soup tastes so deep.

1135
00:41:34,925 --> 00:41:36,427
So he is an old dude, after all.

1136
00:41:36,494 --> 00:41:38,262
He really is.

1137
00:41:38,329 --> 00:41:40,030
Are you using noodles too?

1138
00:41:40,097 --> 00:41:41,198
Yes, I have noodles.

1139
00:41:41,265 --> 00:41:42,600
Those look curly.

1140
00:41:42,900 --> 00:41:44,335
EUN-YEONG'S NOODLES ARE ALMOST DONE

1141
00:41:44,401 --> 00:41:45,903
You should cook your noodles.

1142
00:41:45,970 --> 00:41:48,472
-Did you stir the noodles?
-The noodles.

1143
00:41:49,139 --> 00:41:50,908
BOILING

1144
00:41:50,975 --> 00:41:52,543
It's boiling over!

1145
00:41:52,610 --> 00:41:54,044
The noodles are boiling over!

1146
00:41:54,111 --> 00:41:56,580
-It must be hot!
-Okay.

1147
00:41:56,647 --> 00:41:58,282
-Give me a strainer.
-What?

1148
00:41:59,083 --> 00:42:00,451
A DEPENDABLE HELP TODAY

1149
00:42:00,518 --> 00:42:02,753
The noodles are cooked,
and he uses a strainer.

1150
00:42:02,820 --> 00:42:05,689
-They're like actual ramen noodles.
-It seems so.

1151
00:42:05,756 --> 00:42:08,225
They're using
two distinct types of noodles.

1152
00:42:08,292 --> 00:42:10,294
They're using two different noodles.

1153
00:42:11,862 --> 00:42:13,797
-They look like ramen noodles.
-They look great.

1154
00:42:13,864 --> 00:42:15,165
Do they?

1155
00:42:15,232 --> 00:42:18,035
-It looks like a bowl of ramen.
-And the eggs? Whoa!

1156
00:42:19,203 --> 00:42:20,738
This is insane.

1157
00:42:20,804 --> 00:42:22,373
It looks so crazy.

1158
00:42:23,207 --> 00:42:24,041
SCALLIONS

1159
00:42:25,075 --> 00:42:27,411
Are you using all the noodles?
Are these it? No.

1160
00:42:27,478 --> 00:42:28,712
No, these aren't it.

1161
00:42:28,779 --> 00:42:30,848
I wanted to try the noodles.

1162
00:42:30,915 --> 00:42:32,750
-Hyun-suk's noodles.
-He didn't even look.

1163
00:42:33,183 --> 00:42:34,885
SLURP

1164
00:42:34,952 --> 00:42:37,888
Do the noodles taste rich,
living up to his years of experience?

1165
00:42:38,989 --> 00:42:39,957
The noodles are insane.

1166
00:42:40,024 --> 00:42:41,725
They're insane?

1167
00:42:42,192 --> 00:42:43,427
WHAT ABOUT THE BROTH?

1168
00:42:43,494 --> 00:42:44,595
They're so chewy.

1169
00:42:44,662 --> 00:42:46,330
He's keeping it warm.

1170
00:42:46,397 --> 00:42:47,765
INTO A THERMAL BOTTLE

1171
00:42:49,166 --> 00:42:50,000
No way, Hyun-suk.

1172
00:42:50,067 --> 00:42:51,135
And now…

1173
00:42:51,201 --> 00:42:52,803
He's done!

1174
00:42:52,870 --> 00:42:54,972
He's done! You surprised me.

1175
00:42:55,039 --> 00:42:58,242
He rings the bell in anger.

1176
00:42:58,309 --> 00:43:00,277
-Really?
-He has the soup in a thermal bottle.

1177
00:43:00,344 --> 00:43:02,012
He can pour it right before he eats.

1178
00:43:02,079 --> 00:43:04,181
-Eun-yeong.
-Because the noodles get soggy.

1179
00:43:04,248 --> 00:43:05,182
So he can pour it in?

1180
00:43:05,382 --> 00:43:07,251
A CONSIDERATE WAY OF SERVING NOODLES

1181
00:43:07,318 --> 00:43:08,752
Let me try Eun-yeong's noodles.

1182
00:43:09,753 --> 00:43:11,322
Eun-yeong's noodles.

1183
00:43:11,388 --> 00:43:14,158
-Does it taste like an old dude's noodles?
-It looks good.

1184
00:43:16,026 --> 00:43:18,028
DROPS THE NOODLE

1185
00:43:18,095 --> 00:43:19,396
It's like a magic trick.

1186
00:43:20,097 --> 00:43:21,865
-The paper tapes?
-But reversed.

1187
00:43:21,932 --> 00:43:23,934
Eun-yeong's noodles are so good too.

1188
00:43:24,335 --> 00:43:25,936
MAYBE HE'S JUST HUNGRY…

1189
00:43:26,003 --> 00:43:27,838
Old man, come and hold this.

1190
00:43:27,905 --> 00:43:29,106
-Old man.
-Me?

1191
00:43:29,173 --> 00:43:31,008
-No, old man.
-Old man.

1192
00:43:31,075 --> 00:43:32,643
-Hurry up. Hold this.
-What?

1193
00:43:32,710 --> 00:43:34,812
-Hold this and get it out.
-Okay.

1194
00:43:34,878 --> 00:43:35,846
It's too hot.

1195
00:43:35,913 --> 00:43:38,015
Twenty seconds remain.

1196
00:43:38,082 --> 00:43:39,817
-Hyun-suk is done.
-It's hot.

1197
00:43:39,883 --> 00:43:41,619
-The old Daddy Long Legs.
-Where do you want them?

1198
00:43:41,685 --> 00:43:43,187
-In here, old man.
-That's Hyun-suk!

1199
00:43:43,253 --> 00:43:45,789
SERVING THE NOODLES
THANKS TO THE OLD DADDY LONG LEGS

1200
00:43:45,856 --> 00:43:47,424
-What's that?
-He didn't complain,

1201
00:43:47,491 --> 00:43:49,660
-…but did as she asked.
-What do you need?

1202
00:43:49,727 --> 00:43:51,495
LASTLY, THE SOUP!

1203
00:43:51,562 --> 00:43:53,831
You don't have much time! Six.

1204
00:43:53,897 --> 00:43:55,933
-And this one.
-Five. Four. Three.

1205
00:43:56,567 --> 00:43:58,335
Two. One.

1206
00:43:58,402 --> 00:44:02,039
She's done. Eun-yeong is done too.

1207
00:44:02,406 --> 00:44:03,874
MOVED

1208
00:44:03,941 --> 00:44:05,275
-That was amazing.
-That was just…

1209
00:44:05,342 --> 00:44:07,144
Both dishes look so good.

1210
00:44:07,211 --> 00:44:09,880
Those look so good.

1211
00:44:09,947 --> 00:44:11,448
My hands are cold.

1212
00:44:11,515 --> 00:44:13,250
See? It wasn't just me.

1213
00:44:13,317 --> 00:44:16,153
-What's wrong?
-I wanted to pour the oil, but I couldn't.

1214
00:44:16,220 --> 00:44:18,022
I forgot to use the oil.

1215
00:44:18,088 --> 00:44:20,257
-Should I ask the old guy?
-Ask him for permission.

1216
00:44:20,324 --> 00:44:21,859
I wanted to add this but couldn't.

1217
00:44:21,925 --> 00:44:23,727
GO AHEAD AND DO IT…

1218
00:44:24,294 --> 00:44:27,164
GETS HIS PERMISSION AND ADDS THE OIL

1219
00:44:27,598 --> 00:44:30,234
PIPING HOT SOUP DISHES ARE ON THEIR WAY!

1220
00:44:30,300 --> 00:44:32,469
This is a neck-and-neck round.

1221
00:44:35,339 --> 00:44:37,374
-The soups were amazing.
-I believe you.

1222
00:44:37,741 --> 00:44:38,609
SNIFF

1223
00:44:38,676 --> 00:44:40,010
They look so good.

1224
00:44:40,077 --> 00:44:42,112
They were.

1225
00:44:42,179 --> 00:44:46,083
Both noodle and soup dishes
were made ready for Seok-jin.

1226
00:44:46,550 --> 00:44:47,418
AMAZED

1227
00:44:47,484 --> 00:44:49,486
THEY LOOK LIKE SOMETHING
A GROWN MAN WOULD LOVE

1228
00:44:49,553 --> 00:44:51,221
After I had a taste, I thought,

1229
00:44:51,288 --> 00:44:53,424
"Seok-jin is a lucky guy."

1230
00:44:53,490 --> 00:44:55,325
Please try Hyun-suk's dish first,

1231
00:44:55,392 --> 00:44:57,261
Don't Shrimp Me Because I'm Old.

1232
00:44:57,628 --> 00:45:00,297
RICH SHRIMPY SOUP

1233
00:45:00,364 --> 00:45:01,965
WITH FRESHLY MADE NOODLES TO HIS LIKING

1234
00:45:03,233 --> 00:45:04,702
SEASONED EGG

1235
00:45:04,768 --> 00:45:07,371
THIN FRESH NOODLES

1236
00:45:07,438 --> 00:45:10,407
SEASONED BOILED PORK

1237
00:45:11,241 --> 00:45:14,912
CHEF CHOI HYUN-SUK'S
DON'T SHRIMP ME BECAUSE I'M OLD

1238
00:45:14,978 --> 00:45:17,581
-The noodles look so good.
-He served the soup on the side.

1239
00:45:17,815 --> 00:45:19,116
LURKING…

1240
00:45:19,183 --> 00:45:20,451
What brings you here?

1241
00:45:20,517 --> 00:45:22,486
I wanted to provide the best service.

1242
00:45:22,553 --> 00:45:24,955
And you do not have to mind
that I remade thin noodles

1243
00:45:25,022 --> 00:45:26,690
after making thick ones

1244
00:45:26,757 --> 00:45:28,125
just because you like it this way.

1245
00:45:28,358 --> 00:45:29,727
THE PATRONIZING OLD DUDE

1246
00:45:29,793 --> 00:45:31,628
He reminds him of that again.

1247
00:45:31,695 --> 00:45:34,031
Was it two minutes?
He finished two minutes early.

1248
00:45:34,098 --> 00:45:36,667
In the end, it's great
that we mentioned it.

1249
00:45:36,734 --> 00:45:38,168
You try doing that up there.

1250
00:45:38,235 --> 00:45:39,503
Next time,

1251
00:45:39,570 --> 00:45:41,405
if you want to change it on the spot,

1252
00:45:41,472 --> 00:45:42,506
you make them.

1253
00:45:42,740 --> 00:45:43,807
THE OLD DUDE HOLDS GRUDGES

1254
00:45:43,874 --> 00:45:45,509
I didn't say it. It was Seok-jin.

1255
00:45:45,576 --> 00:45:46,677
GASP!

1256
00:45:46,744 --> 00:45:48,078
Whoa!

1257
00:45:48,779 --> 00:45:51,081
THICK

1258
00:45:51,915 --> 00:45:53,217
It's so rich.

1259
00:45:53,283 --> 00:45:54,651
-It looks so rich.
-Can you smell it?

1260
00:45:54,718 --> 00:45:56,053
How did he do that?

1261
00:45:56,120 --> 00:45:57,921
Will this go into your photo album?

1262
00:45:57,988 --> 00:45:59,056
Of course.

1263
00:46:00,090 --> 00:46:02,693
TAKING A VIDEO TO SHOW THE SOUP'S TEXTURE

1264
00:46:02,760 --> 00:46:03,927
Whoa, it's really…

1265
00:46:05,963 --> 00:46:08,265
I should've taken pictures
of my dishes too.

1266
00:46:08,332 --> 00:46:09,566
Why didn't I think of that?

1267
00:46:10,534 --> 00:46:12,202
REGRETS

1268
00:46:14,238 --> 00:46:16,406
He scoops some soup

1269
00:46:16,473 --> 00:46:18,108
-to check the texture.
-I'll try it now.

1270
00:46:18,175 --> 00:46:19,443
-Thank you.
-Sure.

1271
00:46:19,510 --> 00:46:22,546
Now, the man who lives for noodles.

1272
00:46:22,980 --> 00:46:24,581
SLURP

1273
00:46:25,149 --> 00:46:26,049
Man.

1274
00:46:26,817 --> 00:46:29,453
AMAZED BY THE RICH SOUP

1275
00:46:29,520 --> 00:46:32,556
When the soup is clear and runny,
it tends to lack depth.

1276
00:46:32,623 --> 00:46:35,325
<i>So if you cook the soup</i>
<i>with </i>ramyeon <i>noodles, it makes it thick.</i>

1277
00:46:35,392 --> 00:46:37,461
Then the soup remains
on your tongue for longer

1278
00:46:37,528 --> 00:46:38,896
while having a deeper taste.

1279
00:46:38,962 --> 00:46:42,299
It'll probably add the texture
of what we consider ramen soup.

1280
00:46:42,366 --> 00:46:44,768
The shrimp ramen
you can only eat in Hokkaido.

1281
00:46:44,835 --> 00:46:47,437
The place with an hour and a half's wait
even at midnight.

1282
00:46:47,504 --> 00:46:48,772
I wonder what it's like.

1283
00:46:48,839 --> 00:46:51,175
-What is it like?
-Man.

1284
00:46:51,241 --> 00:46:55,112
I just saw him make the noodles
with my own two eyes,

1285
00:46:55,179 --> 00:46:57,047
-but it's totally…
-Right?

1286
00:46:57,114 --> 00:46:59,683
THE NOODLES CAPTURED HIS APPETITE

1287
00:46:59,750 --> 00:47:01,985
We didn't switch it out
with something else.

1288
00:47:02,052 --> 00:47:04,354
-Let me try the <i>chashu.</i>
-Sure.

1289
00:47:04,421 --> 00:47:06,290
Dip it in the soup before you eat it.

1290
00:47:08,659 --> 00:47:11,161
-It's also seasoned.
-It is.

1291
00:47:11,228 --> 00:47:12,663
He boiled it with kelp seasoning

1292
00:47:12,729 --> 00:47:14,832
<i>and a bunch of dried shrimp,</i>

1293
00:47:14,898 --> 00:47:16,433
-so it's seasoned.
-After that,

1294
00:47:16,500 --> 00:47:19,069
<i>I coated it with seasoned soy sauce</i>
<i>and seared it.</i>

1295
00:47:19,937 --> 00:47:21,471
That sounds so good.

1296
00:47:22,039 --> 00:47:23,307
Back to the soup.

1297
00:47:24,107 --> 00:47:25,676
SAVORING

1298
00:47:25,742 --> 00:47:26,743
ASTONISHED

1299
00:47:26,810 --> 00:47:28,412
Can you feel it
lingering on your tongue?

1300
00:47:28,478 --> 00:47:30,647
-What's it like?
-He couldn't eat it because of the wait.

1301
00:47:30,714 --> 00:47:32,816
One spoonful of soup

1302
00:47:32,883 --> 00:47:34,885
tastes like a stash of shrimp.

1303
00:47:35,152 --> 00:47:36,854
WOW

1304
00:47:36,920 --> 00:47:38,055
I see.

1305
00:47:38,121 --> 00:47:41,225
It tastes like
concentrated shrimp broth.

1306
00:47:41,291 --> 00:47:43,694
-It's a wonder.
-It is?

1307
00:47:43,760 --> 00:47:47,231
Was it called bisque? It tastes like
something that requires lots of work.

1308
00:47:47,297 --> 00:47:50,400
But he created this flavor
in such a short while.

1309
00:47:50,467 --> 00:47:51,602
But that's…

1310
00:47:51,969 --> 00:47:53,203
HE CAN'T STOP EATING

1311
00:47:53,270 --> 00:47:54,104
You can have more later.

1312
00:47:54,171 --> 00:47:56,240
He remade the noodles so it's thinner.

1313
00:47:56,306 --> 00:47:58,075
Do you prefer these thin noodles?

1314
00:47:58,141 --> 00:48:01,278
You know, now that I've had
these thin noodles,

1315
00:48:01,345 --> 00:48:03,580
I think I get what he intended
with the thick ones.

1316
00:48:05,649 --> 00:48:08,619
-Oh, what a shame.
-You should've stuck with it.

1317
00:48:08,852 --> 00:48:11,154
THIN NOODLES, RED RED

1318
00:48:11,221 --> 00:48:13,390
What can you do? That's the final dish.

1319
00:48:13,457 --> 00:48:14,791
What if you lose because of us?

1320
00:48:14,858 --> 00:48:16,627
-That's not our fault.
-Why not?

1321
00:48:16,693 --> 00:48:18,161
He was the one easily convinced.

1322
00:48:18,495 --> 00:48:19,529
PISSED OFF…

1323
00:48:19,596 --> 00:48:21,465
-He didn't ask him to change it.
-No.

1324
00:48:21,531 --> 00:48:22,366
He just said it.

1325
00:48:22,432 --> 00:48:24,134
He should've had some backbone
and stuck with it.

1326
00:48:24,201 --> 00:48:25,769
You should've kept it going.

1327
00:48:25,836 --> 00:48:26,703
Why would you change it?

1328
00:48:26,770 --> 00:48:29,439
-You always do what you intend to.
-Yes, I stick with it.

1329
00:48:29,506 --> 00:48:30,440
-Grow a spine.
-Poong.

1330
00:48:30,707 --> 00:48:31,975
SENSES A THREAT

1331
00:48:32,042 --> 00:48:33,243
-He wants you.
-Kim Poong.

1332
00:48:34,344 --> 00:48:35,178
After the shoot…

1333
00:48:35,245 --> 00:48:36,313
LOOKS AWAY

1334
00:48:36,380 --> 00:48:37,414
I'm busy.

1335
00:48:37,481 --> 00:48:38,882
Wait in the fourth dressing room.

1336
00:48:38,949 --> 00:48:40,350
I'm busy.

1337
00:48:40,584 --> 00:48:42,185
LOL

1338
00:48:42,252 --> 00:48:43,787
I worked so hard on the noodles,

1339
00:48:43,854 --> 00:48:45,522
so please have another spoonful
of the soup.

1340
00:48:46,757 --> 00:48:49,126
He wants to lock him up in his soup.

1341
00:48:49,192 --> 00:48:50,527
CAN HE REALLY LOCK HIM UP?

1342
00:48:50,594 --> 00:48:51,695
One last sip.

1343
00:48:51,762 --> 00:48:53,630
-"Lock him up"?
-Lock him up.

1344
00:48:53,697 --> 00:48:57,567
Next, Eun-yeong's dish, OldSoupSoup.

1345
00:48:58,268 --> 00:49:02,372
HONG KONG-STYLE SHRIMP NOODLE SOUP
PERFECT FOR THE OLD DUDE'S APPETITE

1346
00:49:03,373 --> 00:49:07,210
CHEF PARK EUN-YEONG'S OLDSOUPSOUP

1347
00:49:07,811 --> 00:49:08,946
It looks fancy.

1348
00:49:09,012 --> 00:49:11,281
-This looks crazy too.
-She made fish balls.

1349
00:49:11,348 --> 00:49:13,350
You can tell that it's Hong Kong-style.

1350
00:49:13,417 --> 00:49:16,153
Hong Kong-style shrimp noodle soup

1351
00:49:16,219 --> 00:49:18,555
usually needs fresh shrimp
with their heads intact,

1352
00:49:18,622 --> 00:49:19,790
but you didn't have any,

1353
00:49:19,856 --> 00:49:21,925
so I made one to look like it.

1354
00:49:21,992 --> 00:49:23,493
She made a shrimp.

1355
00:49:23,560 --> 00:49:27,230
<i>She made it with the head and tail,</i>
<i>with the meat in the middle.</i>

1356
00:49:27,431 --> 00:49:28,999
AGAIN, THE SOUP FIRST

1357
00:49:29,066 --> 00:49:32,402
The two dishes are completely different.
Shrimp noodles and ramen.

1358
00:49:32,469 --> 00:49:33,470
Shrimp noodle soup.

1359
00:49:33,537 --> 00:49:34,972
Man.

1360
00:49:35,038 --> 00:49:36,573
A NECK-AND-NECK BATTLE

1361
00:49:36,640 --> 00:49:39,176
-It must be refreshing with the tomatoes.
-It's a close fight.

1362
00:49:39,242 --> 00:49:41,478
And she made it a little spicy.

1363
00:49:41,545 --> 00:49:43,947
Eun-yeong's soup dishes are to die for.

1364
00:49:44,014 --> 00:49:45,649
THE SOUPS ARE EQUALLY GOOD

1365
00:49:45,716 --> 00:49:48,151
They cooked with the same ingredients,
but they're so different.

1366
00:49:48,852 --> 00:49:50,287
The shrimp ball.

1367
00:49:51,421 --> 00:49:53,156
-The shrimp ball.
-That's another kick.

1368
00:49:53,223 --> 00:49:54,658
-The shrimp ball.
-The shrimp ball.

1369
00:49:55,158 --> 00:49:57,461
I'll have to try another one.

1370
00:49:57,694 --> 00:49:58,862
NERVOUS

1371
00:49:58,929 --> 00:50:00,397
These are the same, right?

1372
00:50:00,464 --> 00:50:03,467
Yes, but that one is deep-fried.
The others are boiled.

1373
00:50:03,533 --> 00:50:05,068
<i>That's a deep-fried one.</i>

1374
00:50:05,135 --> 00:50:06,403
-Even better.
-She made those

1375
00:50:06,470 --> 00:50:08,805
and set herself apart from Chef Hyun-suk.

1376
00:50:10,607 --> 00:50:13,944
I can taste the oil in here.

1377
00:50:14,011 --> 00:50:16,146
-The knife-cut noodles.
-They seemed unique and long.

1378
00:50:16,213 --> 00:50:17,280
He had those.

1379
00:50:17,347 --> 00:50:19,416
-Yes.
-And curly.

1380
00:50:19,483 --> 00:50:20,384
SLURP

1381
00:50:22,285 --> 00:50:24,388
HIS MOUTH IS FULL OF NOODLES

1382
00:50:25,188 --> 00:50:26,023
SLURP

1383
00:50:27,624 --> 00:50:28,458
-Gosh.
-Whoa.

1384
00:50:28,525 --> 00:50:29,393
ONE WITH THE NOODLES

1385
00:50:30,327 --> 00:50:32,195
Knife-cut noodles pull in the soup

1386
00:50:32,262 --> 00:50:34,531
as you pick them up to eat.

1387
00:50:34,598 --> 00:50:35,632
-Yes.
-It adds more flavors.

1388
00:50:35,699 --> 00:50:38,468
They didn't look sleek. They had bumps.

1389
00:50:38,535 --> 00:50:41,204
-It had a charm when you bit on it.
-Yes.

1390
00:50:41,705 --> 00:50:42,572
CURIOUS

1391
00:50:42,639 --> 00:50:43,473
Wait.

1392
00:50:43,540 --> 00:50:46,376
What is this scent
that reminds me of Chinese cuisine?

1393
00:50:46,443 --> 00:50:49,446
<i>The noodles came with oil,</i>
<i>so I added that.</i>

1394
00:50:49,513 --> 00:50:52,783
CHINESE-STYLE SPICY LEVEL UP
WITH FLAVORED CHILI OIL

1395
00:50:52,849 --> 00:50:55,886
-It has that numbing <i>mala</i> flavor.
-Like prickly ash.

1396
00:50:55,952 --> 00:50:57,788
Like in <i>malatang.</i>

1397
00:50:58,288 --> 00:51:00,323
It must be chili oil.

1398
00:51:00,390 --> 00:51:01,858
He's in Hong Kong.

1399
00:51:01,925 --> 00:51:04,261
-I'm definitely in Hong Kong.
-And the fish ball.

1400
00:51:04,327 --> 00:51:06,763
Hyun-suk's dish didn't have those.

1401
00:51:07,931 --> 00:51:10,233
-These noodles have their own charm.
-Right?

1402
00:51:10,300 --> 00:51:13,303
So the first noodles were too thin.

1403
00:51:13,370 --> 00:51:15,005
The first noodles were too thin.

1404
00:51:15,072 --> 00:51:17,340
I get why he wanted the noodles thick.

1405
00:51:17,407 --> 00:51:19,342
THE FINISHING BLOW

1406
00:51:19,910 --> 00:51:21,478
-No!
-You should've stuck with it.

1407
00:51:21,545 --> 00:51:23,280
-What now?
-It's the thickness of the noodles.

1408
00:51:23,346 --> 00:51:25,115
My tummy hurts. Please excuse me.

1409
00:51:25,182 --> 00:51:28,118
Don't forget.
We were to make a soup dish, not noodles.

1410
00:51:28,185 --> 00:51:29,853
A soup dish.

1411
00:51:29,920 --> 00:51:31,788
At any rate,
if something goes wrong today,

1412
00:51:31,855 --> 00:51:33,123
many will get hurt.

1413
00:51:35,125 --> 00:51:36,560
IT'S AN EMERGENCY

1414
00:51:36,626 --> 00:51:38,995
-We have to run quickly.
-I have to run.

1415
00:51:39,062 --> 00:51:40,464
Let me get ready to run.

1416
00:51:40,530 --> 00:51:42,265
Come to Break Room Four.

1417
00:51:42,332 --> 00:51:44,034
What break room?

1418
00:51:44,601 --> 00:51:47,404
This soup was rich and velvety,

1419
00:51:47,471 --> 00:51:48,305
whereas this one

1420
00:51:48,371 --> 00:51:50,874
-made me feel refreshed.
-A refreshing touch.

1421
00:51:50,941 --> 00:51:52,976
It felt refreshing
all the way up to my brain.

1422
00:51:53,543 --> 00:51:56,646
When I get hungry at night,
I'd want to eat

1423
00:51:56,713 --> 00:51:58,081
something hearty like this.

1424
00:51:58,148 --> 00:52:00,984
But after having a drink the night before,

1425
00:52:01,051 --> 00:52:05,689
I'd want this as my hangover cure
to get refreshed as I sweat it out.

1426
00:52:05,755 --> 00:52:07,858
-A hangover cure?
-Yes.

1427
00:52:07,924 --> 00:52:11,461
Which soup appealed more
to Seok-jin, the grown man?

1428
00:52:11,528 --> 00:52:13,497
Which one was more captivating?

1429
00:52:13,563 --> 00:52:16,066
Yeo-jeong, you look so happy
as you look at him.

1430
00:52:16,133 --> 00:52:19,336
-I was wondering if I could get a bite.
-Of course.

1431
00:52:19,402 --> 00:52:22,672
Another bowl each, please.

1432
00:52:22,739 --> 00:52:24,207
It must be tough to choose.

1433
00:52:24,274 --> 00:52:26,843
-It's almost painful.
-Right?

1434
00:52:26,910 --> 00:52:28,645
What now?

1435
00:52:28,712 --> 00:52:31,314
-It's soup for a grown man.
-Grown Man's Soup.

1436
00:52:31,381 --> 00:52:33,016
-Yes.
-Oh, no.

1437
00:52:33,083 --> 00:52:34,151
THE SOUP IS HERE

1438
00:52:34,217 --> 00:52:35,218
That soup was so good.

1439
00:52:35,285 --> 00:52:36,253
My gosh. The soup.

1440
00:52:36,319 --> 00:52:38,088
He couldn't make more noodles.

1441
00:52:38,488 --> 00:52:39,489
SMELLS IT FIRST

1442
00:52:39,556 --> 00:52:42,592
He served just the soup for Yeo-jeong.

1443
00:52:42,659 --> 00:52:44,461
-And some meat.
-And meat?

1444
00:52:44,528 --> 00:52:45,729
I'll try this one.

1445
00:52:45,795 --> 00:52:47,731
-That's Chef Hyun-suk's dish.
-Thank you.

1446
00:52:48,598 --> 00:52:50,901
Don't Shrimp Me Because I'm Old.

1447
00:52:50,967 --> 00:52:51,868
GASP!

1448
00:52:52,402 --> 00:52:53,303
CHUCKLE

1449
00:52:53,370 --> 00:52:54,638
A flavor that makes you laugh.

1450
00:52:54,704 --> 00:52:56,973
He made this in ten minutes, right?

1451
00:52:57,040 --> 00:52:59,376
-Exactly.
-Right? Ten minutes or so.

1452
00:52:59,442 --> 00:53:00,944
What on earth is going on?

1453
00:53:01,011 --> 00:53:02,179
<i>Chef & My Fridge.</i>

1454
00:53:02,245 --> 00:53:03,947
IT HAPPENS ON THIS SHOW

1455
00:53:04,014 --> 00:53:07,317
-This is such a wonder.
-She can compare it with Eun-yeong's.

1456
00:53:07,384 --> 00:53:09,686
Hong Kong-style shrimp noodle soup,
OldSoupSoup.

1457
00:53:09,753 --> 00:53:10,620
Wow!

1458
00:53:13,590 --> 00:53:14,691
Your review was perfect.

1459
00:53:14,758 --> 00:53:16,092
-Day and night.
-Night.

1460
00:53:16,159 --> 00:53:17,627
It's so good.

1461
00:53:17,694 --> 00:53:20,597
I'm not so good with spicy food,
but this is just hot enough.

1462
00:53:20,664 --> 00:53:23,200
-Yes, it wasn't too spicy.
-My gosh.

1463
00:53:23,500 --> 00:53:24,467
YUM

1464
00:53:24,968 --> 00:53:26,036
-It must be tough.
-It is.

1465
00:53:26,102 --> 00:53:27,170
It's so good.

1466
00:53:27,237 --> 00:53:28,672
-Is it that good?
-Yes.

1467
00:53:28,738 --> 00:53:30,907
What will you do now?

1468
00:53:30,974 --> 00:53:33,109
-It must be tough to choose.
-I'm stressing out.

1469
00:53:33,176 --> 00:53:34,010
My mind

1470
00:53:34,077 --> 00:53:37,581
keeps changing every second.

1471
00:53:37,647 --> 00:53:39,549
UNPREDICTABLE

1472
00:53:39,616 --> 00:53:42,118
DOESN'T CARE AND TRIES THE PORK

1473
00:53:42,185 --> 00:53:44,588
She tries both soups at the same time.

1474
00:53:44,654 --> 00:53:47,524
She jumps from this soup to that soup.

1475
00:53:47,591 --> 00:53:49,659
I haven't had meat for a while,
and it's so good.

1476
00:53:49,726 --> 00:53:51,962
-Is the pork good?
-So good.

1477
00:53:52,028 --> 00:53:55,298
You can see that Seok-jin
is the only one aging by the minute.

1478
00:53:56,533 --> 00:53:58,501
-Sorry.
-This is how you grow up.

1479
00:53:59,002 --> 00:53:59,836
The OCC.

1480
00:53:59,903 --> 00:54:01,605
You mature this way.

1481
00:54:01,671 --> 00:54:02,806
<i>Press one for Eun-yeong</i>

1482
00:54:03,373 --> 00:54:04,641
<i>and two for Hyun-suk.</i>

1483
00:54:05,175 --> 00:54:07,711
Please make your decision
and press the button!

1484
00:54:08,778 --> 00:54:10,113
Before I do that,

1485
00:54:10,180 --> 00:54:12,482
-let me have them one last time.
-One last time.

1486
00:54:12,549 --> 00:54:14,351
-To leave no regrets.
-It's hard.

1487
00:54:14,417 --> 00:54:16,052
Leaving no regrets.

1488
00:54:16,119 --> 00:54:19,222
What he wanted was a man's soup.

1489
00:54:19,289 --> 00:54:20,390
He should try them again.

1490
00:54:21,324 --> 00:54:22,359
It's not easy.

1491
00:54:22,425 --> 00:54:23,360
EUN-YEONG'S SOUP

1492
00:54:25,562 --> 00:54:27,497
I shouldn't have done that.
It's more confusing.

1493
00:54:27,564 --> 00:54:29,599
-Oh, no.
-He's only getting confused.

1494
00:54:29,766 --> 00:54:30,834
STILL ADDICTED TO THE SOUPS

1495
00:54:30,900 --> 00:54:33,103
Press one for Eun-yeong
and two for Hyun-suk.

1496
00:54:33,169 --> 00:54:34,104
Press the button!

1497
00:54:34,170 --> 00:54:35,372
PRESS

1498
00:54:35,939 --> 00:54:37,807
-I did.
-He did.

1499
00:54:37,874 --> 00:54:39,643
How did you choose?

1500
00:54:39,709 --> 00:54:41,611
Grown Man's Soup.

1501
00:54:41,678 --> 00:54:43,079
Who did he choose?

1502
00:54:43,146 --> 00:54:45,115
Let's find out who Seok-jin chose.

1503
00:54:45,181 --> 00:54:46,416
Show us!

1504
00:54:47,117 --> 00:54:49,919
Hyun-suk's thick and rich soup

1505
00:54:49,986 --> 00:54:51,621
that's perfect for the night

1506
00:54:51,688 --> 00:54:54,391
versus Eun-yeong's refreshingly spicy soup

1507
00:54:54,457 --> 00:54:56,259
that's great for the day.

1508
00:54:56,326 --> 00:54:58,561
Ha Seok-jin, the grown man.

1509
00:54:58,628 --> 00:55:00,930
Which one did he choose?

1510
00:55:00,997 --> 00:55:02,999
Let's find out.

1511
00:55:03,066 --> 00:55:04,968
-Which one?
-Who is it?

1512
00:55:05,035 --> 00:55:06,836
Whose soup did you like better?

1513
00:55:09,906 --> 00:55:11,274
Which one?

1514
00:55:11,341 --> 00:55:12,742
Whose soup did you like better?

1515
00:55:12,809 --> 00:55:13,943
CHOI HYUN-SUK WINS

1516
00:55:14,010 --> 00:55:15,812
DON'T SHRIMP ME BECAUSE I'M OLD WINS

1517
00:55:15,879 --> 00:55:17,047
It was Hyun-suk.

1518
00:55:17,113 --> 00:55:18,448
FLUTTER

1519
00:55:18,515 --> 00:55:19,949
-What a relief.
-Hyun-suk.

1520
00:55:20,016 --> 00:55:22,052
THE GROWN MAN'S PERFORMANCE

1521
00:55:22,118 --> 00:55:24,187
That's the Black Mantis of Geumho-dong.

1522
00:55:25,055 --> 00:55:26,956
-The White Mantis.
-The praying mantis.

1523
00:55:27,023 --> 00:55:28,758
Oh, gosh.

1524
00:55:29,025 --> 00:55:32,462
YOU MUST BE QUITE HAPPY

1525
00:55:32,529 --> 00:55:35,098
When it came to the overall quality,

1526
00:55:35,165 --> 00:55:36,866
<i>Chef Eun-yeong's dish had</i>

1527
00:55:36,933 --> 00:55:41,171
<i>a great balance between</i>
<i>the noodles' texture and the soup.</i>

1528
00:55:41,237 --> 00:55:45,975
So I thought the overall quality
of the dish was higher.

1529
00:55:46,042 --> 00:55:47,977
But when I considered just the soup,

1530
00:55:48,044 --> 00:55:50,780
<i>-I personally prefer something rich.</i>
<i>-His preference.</i>

1531
00:55:50,847 --> 00:55:54,718
So if I were to only consider
the "soup" factor, I liked this better.

1532
00:55:54,784 --> 00:55:57,520
I didn't mention this
in case it affects his decision,

1533
00:55:57,587 --> 00:55:59,189
but I wanted to move in with him.

1534
00:55:59,255 --> 00:56:01,057
-It was that good?
-Yes.

1535
00:56:01,124 --> 00:56:03,860
I wanted to live with him
to eat that soup forever.

1536
00:56:03,927 --> 00:56:05,261
Was that why you hugged him?

1537
00:56:05,328 --> 00:56:08,598
Yes, that was why.
You know I've never done that before.

1538
00:56:08,665 --> 00:56:10,033
As a ramen fan,

1539
00:56:10,100 --> 00:56:14,037
I'm very pleased that I made him
a dish that he has yet to try.

1540
00:56:14,104 --> 00:56:15,004
Above all, all of you.

1541
00:56:15,271 --> 00:56:16,439
WHAT ABOUT US?

1542
00:56:16,506 --> 00:56:17,807
Seok-jin saved you

1543
00:56:17,874 --> 00:56:19,075
from the brink of death.

1544
00:56:19,275 --> 00:56:20,110
WHAT A RELIEF

1545
00:56:20,176 --> 00:56:21,511
PHEW

1546
00:56:21,578 --> 00:56:23,580
Come on, Jung-hwan.

1547
00:56:23,646 --> 00:56:26,082
-The green room almost got shut down.
-Almost got shut down?

1548
00:56:26,149 --> 00:56:27,684
Thank you, Seok-jin.

1549
00:56:27,751 --> 00:56:30,620
-That's his ninth win. Congratulations.
-Congratulations!

1550
00:56:30,687 --> 00:56:32,455
Congratulations, Hyun-suk.

1551
00:56:32,522 --> 00:56:33,356
It was so good.

1552
00:56:33,423 --> 00:56:34,958
Congrats, Hyun-suk.

1553
00:56:35,759 --> 00:56:38,628
AFTER THE FIRST COOK-OFF

1554
00:56:39,229 --> 00:56:40,363
Wasn't it difficult?

1555
00:56:40,430 --> 00:56:43,466
-It was, and they were both so good.
-They were so good.

1556
00:56:43,533 --> 00:56:45,301
-Let me try the soup.
-It's so good.

1557
00:56:45,702 --> 00:56:48,004
IS IT REALLY THAT GOOD?

1558
00:56:48,705 --> 00:56:49,939
YUM

1559
00:56:50,006 --> 00:56:51,341
-Man.
-Right?

1560
00:56:51,408 --> 00:56:52,809
It's really rich.

1561
00:56:53,376 --> 00:56:54,511
Min-hyeong.

1562
00:56:54,711 --> 00:56:56,413
SUMMONS HER MANAGER THIS WEEK AS WELL

1563
00:56:56,479 --> 00:56:58,314
You have to try this one.

1564
00:56:58,381 --> 00:57:00,917
It's insane.

1565
00:57:01,651 --> 00:57:03,686
DRINKING THE SOUP

1566
00:57:04,754 --> 00:57:05,822
It tastes like crab soup.

1567
00:57:06,389 --> 00:57:07,290
Try this too.

1568
00:57:08,958 --> 00:57:09,859
It'll knock you out.

1569
00:57:11,027 --> 00:57:12,829
SLURP

1570
00:57:15,932 --> 00:57:17,634
Isn't it really good?

1571
00:57:17,700 --> 00:57:19,903
-How did he do this in 15 minutes?
-Exactly.

1572
00:57:19,969 --> 00:57:22,839
He actually did it in ten.

1573
00:57:23,973 --> 00:57:25,575
He used a pressure cooker…

1574
00:57:28,545 --> 00:57:29,379
SLURP

1575
00:57:29,446 --> 00:57:32,549
In the end, only Chef Nam-no
and Cho Yeo-jeong stayed behind to eat.

1576
00:57:33,650 --> 00:57:37,854
<i>The second round of cook-off</i>
<i>played with Seok-jin's ingredients.</i>

1577
00:57:37,921 --> 00:57:40,390
The problematic canine, Chef Sam Kim,

1578
00:57:40,457 --> 00:57:44,461
versus the problematic <i>sujebi,</i>
Chef Kwon Sung-jun.

1579
00:57:45,662 --> 00:57:47,096
I received a scandalous tip.

1580
00:57:47,163 --> 00:57:50,266
-What is it?
-It's kind of awkward.

1581
00:57:50,333 --> 00:57:51,601
Sung-jun refused to cook

1582
00:57:51,668 --> 00:57:54,337
unless we shared this news

1583
00:57:54,904 --> 00:57:56,739
-with everyone.
-What is it?

1584
00:57:56,806 --> 00:57:57,974
He made that announcement,

1585
00:57:58,041 --> 00:58:00,443
-so we share this reluctantly.
-Who does he think he is?

1586
00:58:00,510 --> 00:58:03,146
"Chef Sung-jun,
you've been doing great lately,

1587
00:58:03,213 --> 00:58:04,948
with 7 wins and 1 loss after 8 games."

1588
00:58:05,014 --> 00:58:07,517
-Whoa.
-He wanted us to point that out.

1589
00:58:07,784 --> 00:58:08,785
LOL

1590
00:58:08,852 --> 00:58:10,253
He seems so pathetic.

1591
00:58:10,320 --> 00:58:11,221
Are we good now?

1592
00:58:11,287 --> 00:58:13,423
-Yes, we're good.
-Excellent.

1593
00:58:13,490 --> 00:58:15,124
He's been on a roll lately,

1594
00:58:15,191 --> 00:58:18,127
with 7 wins, 1 loss over 8 games.

1595
00:58:18,194 --> 00:58:20,797
He says there's a secret
to this increase in the win rate.

1596
00:58:20,864 --> 00:58:21,898
Is there a secret?

1597
00:58:21,965 --> 00:58:24,501
I began my full-scale hunt
for the stars this year,

1598
00:58:24,567 --> 00:58:25,668
and it wasn't so bad.

1599
00:58:25,735 --> 00:58:27,103
So I want to keep going

1600
00:58:27,170 --> 00:58:29,706
and reach first place in the end.

1601
00:58:29,772 --> 00:58:31,608
-I see.
-How cool. Like how he won <i>CCW.</i>

1602
00:58:31,674 --> 00:58:32,976
So you forgot how to lose?

1603
00:58:33,042 --> 00:58:34,043
How do you lose?

1604
00:58:34,110 --> 00:58:35,612
I'm not like that, am I?

1605
00:58:35,678 --> 00:58:37,013
You were exactly like him.

1606
00:58:37,080 --> 00:58:39,649
-I was?
-Yes, you used to be like that.

1607
00:58:39,716 --> 00:58:42,218
He's who you were ten years ago.

1608
00:58:42,285 --> 00:58:45,054
Chef Nam-no hasn't won
too many stars lately.

1609
00:58:45,121 --> 00:58:47,357
Is there anything
you'd like to share with him?

1610
00:58:47,423 --> 00:58:50,093
Well, there is a secret I hold to.

1611
00:58:50,159 --> 00:58:51,895
But it's not something he can copy anyway.

1612
00:58:52,161 --> 00:58:54,297
SMUG LEVEL

1613
00:58:54,364 --> 00:58:56,366
-He must be pissed.
-I don't want to know anyway!

1614
00:58:56,432 --> 00:58:59,035
-You never know when you'll fight him.
-Right.

1615
00:58:59,102 --> 00:59:00,470
I'll destroy him.

1616
00:59:00,537 --> 00:59:02,005
It'll be funny if you lose today.

1617
00:59:02,071 --> 00:59:03,673
-Oh, no.
-Chef Sam.

1618
00:59:03,740 --> 00:59:07,243
-I want you to win against him.
-Yes, he's been a nuisance.

1619
00:59:07,310 --> 00:59:08,645
Can you please win against him?

1620
00:59:08,711 --> 00:59:09,979
I'll do my best.

1621
00:59:10,947 --> 00:59:12,015
Show your canine today.

1622
00:59:12,815 --> 00:59:14,551
CANINES, PEEK-A-BOO

1623
00:59:14,884 --> 00:59:16,619
A BIG SMILE

1624
00:59:16,886 --> 00:59:18,021
THE GOOD GUY DOES AS HE'S ASKED

1625
00:59:18,087 --> 00:59:19,088
That was so random.

1626
00:59:19,522 --> 00:59:21,624
OFFICIALLY A FAN

1627
00:59:22,258 --> 00:59:23,860
That was lovely.

1628
00:59:23,927 --> 00:59:25,962
-That smile.
-That was nice.

1629
00:59:26,029 --> 00:59:28,565
Sung-jun said this too.

1630
00:59:28,631 --> 00:59:31,034
We recorded another remark.

1631
00:59:31,100 --> 00:59:33,436
"These days, Chef Sam

1632
00:59:33,503 --> 00:59:36,606
-seems to have become my fan."
-What?

1633
00:59:36,673 --> 00:59:38,308
-What do you mean?
-What does this mean?

1634
00:59:38,374 --> 00:59:40,276
He's not the type

1635
00:59:40,343 --> 00:59:42,845
who strikes up a conversation first

1636
00:59:42,912 --> 00:59:44,514
because he's introverted.

1637
00:59:44,581 --> 00:59:47,417
But recently, the way he looks at me
has changed a little.

1638
00:59:47,483 --> 00:59:48,918
I see. How so?

1639
00:59:48,985 --> 00:59:50,520
He finds me endearing.

1640
00:59:50,587 --> 00:59:51,688
-He does?
-Yes.

1641
00:59:51,754 --> 00:59:54,190
I think he's studying me like a fan.

1642
00:59:55,358 --> 00:59:56,759
-Maybe he was dumbfounded.
-He has fallen.

1643
00:59:56,826 --> 00:59:58,962
-I think he fell for my charm.
-He fell for your charm.

1644
00:59:59,028 --> 00:59:59,996
It's not easy to leave.

1645
01:00:00,063 --> 01:00:01,497
I see. Sure.

1646
01:00:01,564 --> 01:00:02,832
-Is it true?
-Is that so, Sam?

1647
01:00:02,899 --> 01:00:04,500
-He's right.
-Why?

1648
01:00:04,701 --> 01:00:05,969
OH MY

1649
01:00:06,035 --> 01:00:07,604
When we first met,

1650
01:00:07,670 --> 01:00:09,505
I thought, "Who the heck is this guy?"

1651
01:00:10,640 --> 01:00:11,941
He said it nicely.

1652
01:00:12,008 --> 01:00:14,077
-I was like, "Who is that punk?"
-Me too.

1653
01:00:14,143 --> 01:00:16,546
-Everyone had that thought.
-But I caught on to his charm,

1654
01:00:16,613 --> 01:00:17,547
and one day,

1655
01:00:17,614 --> 01:00:20,149
I realized that I could predict
his psychological state

1656
01:00:20,216 --> 01:00:22,852
by looking at him
and listening to his words.

1657
01:00:22,919 --> 01:00:24,220
-I see.
-So it's fun.

1658
01:00:24,287 --> 01:00:25,788
He's having fun.

1659
01:00:25,855 --> 01:00:27,991
-Look at him now.
-What's on his mind?

1660
01:00:28,057 --> 01:00:29,892
What does that face mean?

1661
01:00:29,959 --> 01:00:30,960
That was appalling.

1662
01:00:31,828 --> 01:00:33,229
He seems quite nervous.

1663
01:00:34,397 --> 01:00:35,765
-Despite what he said?
-Yes.

1664
01:00:35,832 --> 01:00:37,867
-So he's nervous.
-He doesn't want to lose to Sam.

1665
01:00:37,934 --> 01:00:40,436
-He got me.
-Does he get smug when he's nervous?

1666
01:00:40,503 --> 01:00:42,138
That's his coping mechanism.

1667
01:00:43,006 --> 01:00:44,407
-Yes.
-You were bluffing.

1668
01:00:44,474 --> 01:00:45,708
It's been a while

1669
01:00:45,775 --> 01:00:48,011
since we've had the Italian derby.

1670
01:00:48,077 --> 01:00:51,948
Seok-jin will be playing
an Italian chef in his upcoming project,

1671
01:00:52,015 --> 01:00:56,586
so he'll probably keep a keen eye
on the way you two cook.

1672
01:00:56,653 --> 01:01:00,456
Will they get extra points
for sharing some skills you can use?

1673
01:01:00,523 --> 01:01:02,859
If there's something I can pick up.

1674
01:01:02,925 --> 01:01:04,594
-They get extra points?
-Yes.

1675
01:01:04,661 --> 01:01:07,263
I'm sure it'll be very helpful for him.

1676
01:01:08,197 --> 01:01:10,166
CHEF SAM'S INGREDIENTS:
STRIP LOIN, PORK BELLY, BEEF BRISKET

1677
01:01:10,233 --> 01:01:12,268
SCALLION, MUSHROOM CREAM SAUCE,
VEGGIE BROTH, CILANTRO, AND MORE

1678
01:01:12,335 --> 01:01:14,771
The dish I prepared today is

1679
01:01:14,837 --> 01:01:16,906
Beef WellingNull.

1680
01:01:16,973 --> 01:01:18,975
-Beef WellingNull?
-WellingNull?

1681
01:01:19,042 --> 01:01:20,777
-Null?
-Null?

1682
01:01:20,843 --> 01:01:21,711
No?

1683
01:01:21,778 --> 01:01:26,315
Beef Wellington is made
by wrapping beef with ham.

1684
01:01:26,382 --> 01:01:27,884
Instead of ham,

1685
01:01:27,950 --> 01:01:30,820
-I'll use beef brisket instead.
-That's new.

1686
01:01:30,887 --> 01:01:33,656
So I'll use every single meat he has

1687
01:01:33,723 --> 01:01:37,060
and make beef Wellington,
while bringing out the Western flavors.

1688
01:01:37,126 --> 01:01:38,594
Why is it WellingNull?

1689
01:01:38,661 --> 01:01:41,864
-I'll be emptying out his fridge.
-His fridge.

1690
01:01:41,931 --> 01:01:43,032
-Nullifying.
-I get it.

1691
01:01:43,099 --> 01:01:45,034
-He'll empty it.
-He'll empty it for me.

1692
01:01:45,968 --> 01:01:47,970
CHEF SUNG-JUN'S INGREDIENTS:
PORK BELLY, PORK BELLY END CUT, STRIP LOIN

1693
01:01:48,037 --> 01:01:50,039
BEEF TONGUE, OYSTER SAUCE, SOY SAUCE,
HOT SAUCE, WHITE BALSAMIC, AND MORE

1694
01:01:50,106 --> 01:01:51,808
What I'll make today is called

1695
01:01:52,442 --> 01:01:54,277
<i>Mantecare</i> on the Menu.

1696
01:01:54,343 --> 01:01:56,279
-I see.
-<i>Mantecare</i> on the Menu.

1697
01:01:56,345 --> 01:01:59,615
Not <i>Love on the Menu</i>,
but <i>Mantecare</i> on the Menu.

1698
01:01:59,682 --> 01:02:03,352
<i>Mantecare</i> is an Italian word
that means "to mix."

1699
01:02:03,419 --> 01:02:06,756
-So you mix your food by shaking the wok.
-And quickly too.

1700
01:02:07,557 --> 01:02:08,891
I'll show you that.

1701
01:02:08,958 --> 01:02:11,194
I'm going to make pasta
in the style of <i>mazesoba.</i>

1702
01:02:11,260 --> 01:02:12,095
That sounds good.

1703
01:02:12,161 --> 01:02:14,197
-But it's meat…
-You had lots of meat,

1704
01:02:14,263 --> 01:02:16,099
-…so I plan to use everything.
-Everything.

1705
01:02:16,165 --> 01:02:18,234
I'll grind the meat

1706
01:02:18,301 --> 01:02:20,103
and make a sauce similar to ragu

1707
01:02:20,169 --> 01:02:22,572
and add a variety of toppings.

1708
01:02:22,638 --> 01:02:25,708
Also, it only took me three minutes
to make fresh pasta last time.

1709
01:02:26,909 --> 01:02:28,111
SMUG

1710
01:02:28,177 --> 01:02:31,047
So today, within 2 minutes and 30 seconds,

1711
01:02:31,114 --> 01:02:33,649
-I'll make it happen.
-You're making fresh pasta?

1712
01:02:33,716 --> 01:02:37,086
Also, there's a Japanese dish
which you make with leftover meat

1713
01:02:37,153 --> 01:02:40,690
called <i>menchi katsu, </i>ground meat cutlet,
made with ground meat

1714
01:02:40,757 --> 01:02:42,358
shaped into a ball, then deep-fried.

1715
01:02:42,425 --> 01:02:45,027
So I'll make that as well,
with a bit of an Italian kick.

1716
01:02:46,996 --> 01:02:48,564
Italian meets Japanese.

1717
01:02:48,631 --> 01:02:50,633
Can Seok-jin pick up

1718
01:02:50,700 --> 01:02:54,837
on that skill, <i>mantecare</i>,
by watching you cook?

1719
01:02:54,904 --> 01:02:56,372
It's not just <i>mantecare.</i>

1720
01:02:56,439 --> 01:02:59,509
You'll have to easily make
fresh pasta within two, three minutes

1721
01:02:59,575 --> 01:03:02,211
to call yourself an Italian chef.

1722
01:03:03,079 --> 01:03:04,814
He's quite nervous right now.

1723
01:03:04,881 --> 01:03:06,415
IN A NERVOUS STATE

1724
01:03:06,482 --> 01:03:07,917
He's analyzing him.

1725
01:03:07,984 --> 01:03:09,786
-My heart is racing.
-An interpreter.

1726
01:03:09,852 --> 01:03:11,087
And now,

1727
01:03:11,154 --> 01:03:13,523
let us begin their cook-off.

1728
01:03:13,589 --> 01:03:15,491
This is a meat round,
titled Problematic Meat.

1729
01:03:15,558 --> 01:03:18,227
The man who forgot how to lose.

1730
01:03:18,294 --> 01:03:21,264
The <i>sujebi</i> of ambition,
Chef Kwon Sung-jun.

1731
01:03:21,330 --> 01:03:22,498
I'LL PICK THAT UP!

1732
01:03:22,565 --> 01:03:26,035
He's here to give him a special lecture
on how to lose the cook-off.

1733
01:03:26,102 --> 01:03:29,972
The canine of ambition, Chef Sam Kim.

1734
01:03:30,039 --> 01:03:31,340
"The canine of ambition"?

1735
01:03:31,407 --> 01:03:34,577
Who'll rise as the winner
after this Italian derby?

1736
01:03:34,644 --> 01:03:36,646
Let the 15-minute cook-off begin!

1737
01:03:36,712 --> 01:03:38,247
Let's go!

1738
01:03:38,681 --> 01:03:40,750
SUJEBI VS. CANINE

1739
01:03:40,817 --> 01:03:44,554
-They're using meat.
-Is that pork belly?

1740
01:03:45,488 --> 01:03:47,390
They're both prepping meat.

1741
01:03:48,224 --> 01:03:50,426
1. ROAST SEASONED PORK BELLY IN THE OVEN

1742
01:03:50,493 --> 01:03:51,494
Why is the oven off?

1743
01:03:51,561 --> 01:03:52,695
-Why is it off?
-What?

1744
01:03:52,762 --> 01:03:54,630
-What? Who turned off the oven?
-Emergency.

1745
01:03:54,697 --> 01:03:56,132
-Wait.
-The oven?

1746
01:03:56,199 --> 01:03:57,233
-Who is it?
-The oven was off.

1747
01:03:57,567 --> 01:03:58,434
IT WASN'T ME

1748
01:03:58,501 --> 01:04:00,169
Maybe Chef Canine.

1749
01:04:00,236 --> 01:04:01,537
Who turned off the oven?

1750
01:04:01,604 --> 01:04:03,506
I'm prepping strip loin right now.

1751
01:04:03,573 --> 01:04:06,409
I'm going to mix
beef with a bit of pork belly.

1752
01:04:06,475 --> 01:04:08,744
-Who did it?
-It wasn't me.

1753
01:04:08,811 --> 01:04:10,046
Borrow the one on the right.

1754
01:04:10,112 --> 01:04:10,980
It's okay.

1755
01:04:11,047 --> 01:04:12,215
You can use it.

1756
01:04:12,281 --> 01:04:13,883
You can use Sam's oven, right?

1757
01:04:13,950 --> 01:04:15,985
He's grinding beef right now.

1758
01:04:16,052 --> 01:04:17,587
-Emergency.
-He's using ground meat.

1759
01:04:17,653 --> 01:04:18,487
OFF TO A SHAKY START

1760
01:04:18,554 --> 01:04:20,356
They're both grinding something.

1761
01:04:20,423 --> 01:04:22,792
-Is that for the pasta sauce?
-Yes.

1762
01:04:22,859 --> 01:04:24,627
I'm grinding the meat first.

1763
01:04:24,694 --> 01:04:25,728
BUT HE OVERCOMES IT BRAVELY!

1764
01:04:26,295 --> 01:04:28,197
What's he doing?

1765
01:04:28,264 --> 01:04:31,267
-The dough?
-Yes, to make beef Wellington.

1766
01:04:31,334 --> 01:04:34,036
-To wrap the beef?
-The flour dough.

1767
01:04:34,604 --> 01:04:37,306
Did you add eggs? Or butter?

1768
01:04:37,840 --> 01:04:39,575
He added melted butter.

1769
01:04:39,942 --> 01:04:41,377
GROUND MEAT & PIE DOUGH

1770
01:04:41,444 --> 01:04:43,246
Sam ground two different ingredients.

1771
01:04:44,013 --> 01:04:45,715
First, the melted butter…

1772
01:04:45,781 --> 01:04:47,717
He's seasoning the meat.

1773
01:04:48,117 --> 01:04:49,485
TAP TAP TAP!

1774
01:04:49,552 --> 01:04:52,355
What he's doing
to season the meat is correct.

1775
01:04:52,421 --> 01:04:54,190
You always start with ground pepper.

1776
01:04:54,257 --> 01:04:56,459
Always start with the coarser ingredient.

1777
01:04:56,525 --> 01:04:58,961
-I see.
-I'm a chef, so when I watch dramas,

1778
01:04:59,028 --> 01:05:01,397
wrong details get on my nerves.

1779
01:05:04,233 --> 01:05:06,235
PORK BELLY END CUT, STRIP LOIN

1780
01:05:06,569 --> 01:05:08,571
BEEF TONGUE (FOR BULGOGI)

1781
01:05:08,971 --> 01:05:10,840
WATCH CLOSELY!

1782
01:05:11,073 --> 01:05:12,642
-Whoa.
-Yes.

1783
01:05:12,708 --> 01:05:13,910
Look at him chop.

1784
01:05:13,976 --> 01:05:15,878
COOK LIKE A CHEF
LESSON 3: KEEP YOUR HEAD AT 45°

1785
01:05:15,945 --> 01:05:16,946
That's Sung-jun.

1786
01:05:17,013 --> 01:05:19,348
He's been on a roll lately.

1787
01:05:19,415 --> 01:05:21,284
-He's been doing great lately.
-Yes.

1788
01:05:21,350 --> 01:05:23,085
He's chopping an onion too.
Don't let him win!

1789
01:05:23,152 --> 01:05:24,687
Look at Sam go.

1790
01:05:24,754 --> 01:05:26,622
That's how Italian chefs chop.

1791
01:05:26,689 --> 01:05:28,891
-He's good.
-Yes, that's it.

1792
01:05:29,125 --> 01:05:30,693
A GREAT CHANCE TO OBSERVE!

1793
01:05:30,760 --> 01:05:32,328
It sounds so rhythmical.

1794
01:05:33,095 --> 01:05:33,996
OLIVE OIL, GARLIC

1795
01:05:34,797 --> 01:05:36,198
SCALLIONS

1796
01:05:36,265 --> 01:05:38,000
2. COOK ONION, GARLIC, SCALLION
IN OLIVE OIL

1797
01:05:39,068 --> 01:05:40,369
SUNG-JUN'S TAKE ON THE TOPIC

1798
01:05:40,436 --> 01:05:42,939
He'll make the sauce similar to ragu
and serve it like mixed noodles.

1799
01:05:43,005 --> 01:05:45,274
GROUND THE PROBLEMATIC MEAT
AND USE IT AS SAUCE!

1800
01:05:45,341 --> 01:05:46,943
What's he adding? Garlic?

1801
01:05:47,009 --> 01:05:47,877
GASP

1802
01:05:47,944 --> 01:05:48,911
That was a lot of salt.

1803
01:05:48,978 --> 01:05:50,479
Didn't you pour in too much salt?

1804
01:05:50,546 --> 01:05:51,814
No, it needs this much.

1805
01:05:51,881 --> 01:05:53,482
It needs this much.

1806
01:05:53,549 --> 01:05:55,718
-Did you use garlic, Sung-jun?
-Garlic.

1807
01:05:55,785 --> 01:05:57,720
-Garlic, ginger, and onion.
-And ginger?

1808
01:05:57,787 --> 01:05:59,689
-And ginger?
-Their processes are similar.

1809
01:05:59,755 --> 01:06:02,892
-They're both using onion and meat.
-The pans look similar.

1810
01:06:02,959 --> 01:06:03,793
A similar process.

1811
01:06:05,828 --> 01:06:07,096
White balsamic vinegar.

1812
01:06:07,163 --> 01:06:08,731
-Balsamic vinegar?
-Balsamic vinegar?

1813
01:06:08,798 --> 01:06:09,632
White balsamic vinegar.

1814
01:06:09,699 --> 01:06:11,434
It adds sourness to the pasta.

1815
01:06:11,500 --> 01:06:13,936
That too, but it's also sweet.
That's a delicious sauce.

1816
01:06:14,003 --> 01:06:15,538
-I see.
-White balsamic vinegar.

1817
01:06:15,604 --> 01:06:16,973
-White balsamic.
-Yes.

1818
01:06:17,239 --> 01:06:19,342
SAM'S TAKE ON THE TOPIC!

1819
01:06:19,976 --> 01:06:21,777
-That looks good.
-That looks delicious.

1820
01:06:21,844 --> 01:06:23,312
I want a bite of that meat.

1821
01:06:23,379 --> 01:06:24,814
Keep it up, Sam.

1822
01:06:24,880 --> 01:06:27,183
-That must be good meat.
-It is.

1823
01:06:27,249 --> 01:06:29,819
He couldn't eat it alone,

1824
01:06:29,885 --> 01:06:31,787
so he thought it was such a waste.

1825
01:06:31,854 --> 01:06:34,490
-I was afraid of the security guard.
-Yes, of course.

1826
01:06:36,559 --> 01:06:37,393
MUSHROOM CREAM SAUCE

1827
01:06:37,860 --> 01:06:38,694
VEGGIE BROTH

1828
01:06:38,761 --> 01:06:39,929
3. COOK GROUND BEEF, SAUCE, BROTH

1829
01:06:40,463 --> 01:06:41,731
MAKING THE PASTA DOUGH!

1830
01:06:41,797 --> 01:06:43,733
-The dough?
-The pasta dough.

1831
01:06:43,799 --> 01:06:45,334
-That's fast.
-You're using your hand?

1832
01:06:46,235 --> 01:06:47,503
He's kneading with his hand.

1833
01:06:48,671 --> 01:06:50,373
-And not a blender?
-Not a blender.

1834
01:06:50,439 --> 01:06:52,108
-The pasta dough?
-Yes.

1835
01:06:52,174 --> 01:06:54,310
Is Sam making noodles too?

1836
01:06:54,377 --> 01:06:57,613
Wellington's… WellingNull's wrap.

1837
01:06:58,647 --> 01:07:00,282
Why are you using your hands?

1838
01:07:00,349 --> 01:07:01,984
Italian chefs
aren't allowed to use machines.

1839
01:07:02,218 --> 01:07:03,419
REALLY?

1840
01:07:03,486 --> 01:07:05,287
You must do everything by hand.

1841
01:07:05,354 --> 01:07:08,724
-But Hyun-suk used a tool.
-He did.

1842
01:07:08,791 --> 01:07:09,859
He's not an Italian chef?

1843
01:07:10,993 --> 01:07:11,894
He's a chef.

1844
01:07:11,961 --> 01:07:14,497
-Whoa, already?
-It's already coming out.

1845
01:07:14,563 --> 01:07:17,967
-It's rather yellow.
-Take your time. It's okay.

1846
01:07:18,868 --> 01:07:20,403
Isn't that a tool?

1847
01:07:20,469 --> 01:07:22,938
What's that, then? Your palms?

1848
01:07:23,572 --> 01:07:25,941
-Do you consider that your hand?
-Answer him.

1849
01:07:26,008 --> 01:07:27,410
He never answers if it's to his detriment.

1850
01:07:28,778 --> 01:07:31,714
Wait a minute. It doesn't seem so smooth.

1851
01:07:31,781 --> 01:07:33,582
Wait a second. Is that okay?

1852
01:07:33,649 --> 01:07:35,117
He's like me in the last episode.

1853
01:07:36,585 --> 01:07:38,020
Should I hold it?

1854
01:07:40,656 --> 01:07:42,992
He has a minute to finish up.

1855
01:07:43,059 --> 01:07:44,760
A minute until the time he promised.

1856
01:07:44,827 --> 01:07:46,195
-A minute?
-Yes.

1857
01:07:46,262 --> 01:07:47,329
I only need 30 seconds.

1858
01:07:47,396 --> 01:07:49,932
He kept running it through,
so it must be chewy.

1859
01:07:49,999 --> 01:07:51,067
It works well now.

1860
01:07:52,201 --> 01:07:54,036
IT'S COMING OUT!

1861
01:07:54,603 --> 01:07:55,504
Done.

1862
01:07:55,571 --> 01:07:57,606
-You're done already?
-I'm done.

1863
01:07:57,673 --> 01:07:59,708
He did it 30 seconds faster.

1864
01:07:59,775 --> 01:08:01,510
It took him two minutes.

1865
01:08:01,577 --> 01:08:03,412
Two minutes? How did I do it so quickly?

1866
01:08:03,479 --> 01:08:05,448
Seok-jin, is it thin enough?

1867
01:08:05,514 --> 01:08:06,649
UM

1868
01:08:06,715 --> 01:08:07,883
They look fine.

1869
01:08:07,950 --> 01:08:09,518
Thicker pasta may be better.

1870
01:08:09,585 --> 01:08:10,419
Aren't they too thin?

1871
01:08:10,486 --> 01:08:12,354
They aren't. Not at all.

1872
01:08:12,621 --> 01:08:13,589
CHECKING FOR TIME

1873
01:08:13,923 --> 01:08:15,157
CREAM AND MEAT MIXTURE

1874
01:08:15,524 --> 01:08:19,562
4. DRAIN OVER A STRAINER

1875
01:08:19,895 --> 01:08:22,298
THE BROTH WILL TURN INTO SAUCE!

1876
01:08:23,265 --> 01:08:24,900
NEXT, FLATTEN THE DOUGH THINLY

1877
01:08:25,167 --> 01:08:26,969
What's Sam up to?

1878
01:08:27,036 --> 01:08:28,437
Me? I'll wrap this now.

1879
01:08:28,504 --> 01:08:30,005
Mustard?

1880
01:08:30,072 --> 01:08:31,040
Mustard.

1881
01:08:31,107 --> 01:08:34,477
-He's using mustard.
-The one he asked you to use.

1882
01:08:34,543 --> 01:08:36,579
It should be in the oven already.

1883
01:08:36,645 --> 01:08:38,647
-Yes, the Wellington.
-You have no time.

1884
01:08:38,714 --> 01:08:39,715
Eight minutes.

1885
01:08:41,317 --> 01:08:42,485
It's okay.

1886
01:08:43,185 --> 01:08:44,019
TRUFFLE-FLAVORED MUSTARD

1887
01:08:44,086 --> 01:08:46,555
He topped the beef brisket
with strip loin.

1888
01:08:46,622 --> 01:08:47,656
You're right.

1889
01:08:47,923 --> 01:08:48,858
CREAM AND MEAT MIXTURE

1890
01:08:49,492 --> 01:08:51,927
-It's quite thick.
-Does he have enough time?

1891
01:08:51,994 --> 01:08:53,262
Why don't you sear it first?

1892
01:08:53,329 --> 01:08:54,964
No. It's okay.

1893
01:08:55,631 --> 01:08:56,699
Will it get fully cooked?

1894
01:08:56,765 --> 01:08:59,335
He cooked the strip loin
in case it doesn't.

1895
01:08:59,401 --> 01:09:01,537
-I see.
-And beef brisket cooks quickly.

1896
01:09:01,604 --> 01:09:04,340
He's cooking it in a pan first.

1897
01:09:04,406 --> 01:09:05,875
Okay.

1898
01:09:05,941 --> 01:09:08,344
-He's cooking it first.
-Eight minutes left.

1899
01:09:08,410 --> 01:09:11,280
Can you ground the sesame seeds?

1900
01:09:11,347 --> 01:09:13,082
-This one?
-Sesame seeds.

1901
01:09:14,884 --> 01:09:18,521
He's grinding the bread
from Seok-jin's sister.

1902
01:09:18,587 --> 01:09:20,689
-Really?
-The German bread.

1903
01:09:20,756 --> 01:09:23,359
He's using that bread. No way.

1904
01:09:23,425 --> 01:09:24,927
As breadcrumbs, probably.

1905
01:09:24,994 --> 01:09:26,495
As breadcrumbs. Yes.

1906
01:09:27,229 --> 01:09:29,165
GROUND BEEF TONGUE AND ONION

1907
01:09:29,365 --> 01:09:30,733
SOY SAUCE, OYSTER SAUCE, MILK

1908
01:09:30,799 --> 01:09:32,635
Is that milk? Did you add milk?

1909
01:09:32,701 --> 01:09:33,736
For ground beef cutlets.

1910
01:09:33,802 --> 01:09:36,238
People do this in Italy
to make the meat tenderer.

1911
01:09:37,006 --> 01:09:38,607
It's the Italian method.

1912
01:09:38,674 --> 01:09:40,209
I don't think you added enough.

1913
01:09:40,276 --> 01:09:41,243
That was a tiny dash.

1914
01:09:41,310 --> 01:09:43,078
-Did he not have enough?
-Was it not enough?

1915
01:09:43,145 --> 01:09:44,647
No, not too much. Less is more.

1916
01:09:44,713 --> 01:09:45,781
He's really fast.

1917
01:09:45,848 --> 01:09:47,816
By less, do you mean milk?

1918
01:09:47,883 --> 01:09:48,717
Sure.

1919
01:09:50,419 --> 01:09:51,820
Great question.

1920
01:09:51,887 --> 01:09:53,255
And a good answer.

1921
01:09:53,322 --> 01:09:54,423
That makes sense.

1922
01:09:54,490 --> 01:09:56,825
The meat is coming undone.

1923
01:09:56,892 --> 01:09:58,460
Poong, look at him.

1924
01:09:58,527 --> 01:09:59,895
His Wellington is falling apart.

1925
01:09:59,962 --> 01:10:01,564
Beef brisket is coming undone!

1926
01:10:01,630 --> 01:10:04,266
That can't happen
when you make a Wellington!

1927
01:10:04,333 --> 01:10:06,635
-It's coming undone!
-It's okay.

1928
01:10:06,702 --> 01:10:08,070
Is he serious?

1929
01:10:08,137 --> 01:10:09,905
You should do it over.

1930
01:10:09,972 --> 01:10:12,841
You're awful, Hyun-suk.
Why did you put him up for it?

1931
01:10:12,908 --> 01:10:14,877
He just made Poong do that.

1932
01:10:14,944 --> 01:10:17,213
I thought he was telling him to help.

1933
01:10:17,279 --> 01:10:18,581
Why did you make me do it?

1934
01:10:19,381 --> 01:10:20,516
You're mean.

1935
01:10:20,583 --> 01:10:22,651
You two are the same.

1936
01:10:23,619 --> 01:10:24,787
You're both mean.

1937
01:10:24,853 --> 01:10:26,055
They're so funny.

1938
01:10:26,121 --> 01:10:26,956
Things are heating up.

1939
01:10:28,190 --> 01:10:30,759
Sung-jun is making another batter.

1940
01:10:30,826 --> 01:10:33,329
He'll dip the cutlet in that
before the breadcrumbs.

1941
01:10:34,163 --> 01:10:35,097
The batter.

1942
01:10:35,164 --> 01:10:36,565
He'll dip the meat in that first.

1943
01:10:36,632 --> 01:10:38,500
And not just breadcrumbs?
Is that the recipe?

1944
01:10:38,567 --> 01:10:39,568
That's the way to do it.

1945
01:10:39,635 --> 01:10:40,703
For ground beef cutlets?

1946
01:10:40,769 --> 01:10:42,805
COOK LIKE A CHEF
LESSON 4: BIG AND QUICK ACTION!

1947
01:10:42,871 --> 01:10:44,673
Do you see this, Jee Seok-jin?

1948
01:10:44,740 --> 01:10:46,342
Do you see this, Jee Seok-jin?

1949
01:10:46,408 --> 01:10:47,243
He's Ha Seok-jin.

1950
01:10:47,309 --> 01:10:48,711
Wait, Jee? I meant Ha Seok-jin.

1951
01:10:48,777 --> 01:10:50,946
We're hearing his name often today.

1952
01:10:51,013 --> 01:10:52,381
FAREWELL, PIZZA, WE WON'T SEE YOU OFF

1953
01:10:52,448 --> 01:10:53,849
-Minus five points!
-Come on!

1954
01:10:53,916 --> 01:10:55,851
Ha Seok-jin!

1955
01:10:58,921 --> 01:11:00,089
It's an emergency.

1956
01:11:00,356 --> 01:11:01,757
A BIG MISS…

1957
01:11:01,824 --> 01:11:04,159
Where's the emergency light?

1958
01:11:04,226 --> 01:11:05,527
I'm sorry, Ha Seok-jin.

1959
01:11:05,594 --> 01:11:08,297
You have six minutes to go.

1960
01:11:08,364 --> 01:11:09,398
The pasta? Already?

1961
01:11:10,165 --> 01:11:12,668
Pasta made with eggs
will cook really quickly.

1962
01:11:13,802 --> 01:11:16,739
Sam put the meat on the pie dough.

1963
01:11:17,640 --> 01:11:19,808
That's the Wellington.

1964
01:11:19,875 --> 01:11:22,311
Seok-jin has his eyes on the chefs.

1965
01:11:24,013 --> 01:11:25,014
CHECKING FOR TIME

1966
01:11:25,080 --> 01:11:28,350
He has to stick it in the oven
to let the dough get cooked.

1967
01:11:28,417 --> 01:11:32,054
-Yes.
-Just flatten it with your hands.

1968
01:11:32,121 --> 01:11:33,289
Wow.

1969
01:11:33,355 --> 01:11:35,491
As you watch him, you'll probably realize

1970
01:11:35,557 --> 01:11:37,426
